// A TLS 1.3 client (RFC 8446), with a TLS 1.2 fallback (RFC 5246) that the
// server alone can pick, that checks who it is talking to, by pinned key or by
// certificate chain, and has no way not to.
//
// API
// ---
//   tls.connect(host, port, trust)   -> Result<Conn, Error>
//   tls.connect_at(host, port, trust, now) -> Result<Conn, Error>   the clock given, in epoch seconds
//   tls.connect_over(socket, host, trust)  -> Result<Conn, Error>   over a net.Conn already open
//   tls.connect_over_at(socket, host, trust, now)                   the same, the clock given
//   tls.connect_over_deadline(socket, host, trust, deadline_ms, timeout_ms: 0)
//                                         a total bound on the handshake (see below)
//   tls.pinned_key(sha256)           -> Trust            from 32 octets
//   tls.pinned_key_hex(text)         -> Option<Trust>    from 64 hex digits
//   tls.Trust.SystemRoots            the operating system's CA bundle
//   tls.Trust.CaFile(path)           the roots in this PEM file
//
//   c.read(n), c.read_all(), c.read_until(delimiter, limit)
//   c.write(data), c.close()                  `io.Stream`'s five methods
//   c.last_error()                   -> Option<Error>    why the connection died
//   c.peer_alert()                   -> int              the alert it sent, or -1
//
// A `Conn` is an `io.Stream`, so everything written against that protocol
// runs on it unchanged: `http.write_request(conn, ...)` and
// `http.read_response(conn, ...)` need no `https` branch.
//
// --- trust --------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `Trust` says whom the server must prove it is. It has three variants:
//
// `PinnedKey` is the SHA-256 of the leaf certificate's DER
// SubjectPublicKeyInfo -- what `openssl x509 -pubkey -noout | openssl pkey
// -pubin -outform DER | openssl dgst -sha256` prints, and what HPKP and
// curl's `--pinnedpubkey` call a pin. The certificate is *not* otherwise
// examined under a pin: no chain, no expiry, no host name. The pin names the
// key, `host` is only where to connect and what to put in SNI, and a
// certificate that is expired or issued to another name is accepted if its
// key is the one pinned.
//
// `SystemRoots` and `CaFile(path)` validate the whole chain
// (`lib/x509chain.m31` has the rules): a path from the server's certificate
// through whatever intermediates it sent to a root in the bundle, every
// signature verified, every validity period holding at connection time, the
// CA and path-length constraints, serverAuth, and the leaf's subjectAltName
// covering `host`. `CaFile` reads the roots from one PEM file. `SystemRoots`
// reads the first of these that can be read:
//
//   $SSL_CERT_FILE                      when set, and then only this one: an
//                                       override that names an unreadable
//                                       file is an error, not a reason to
//                                       trust something else
//   /etc/pki/tls/certs/ca-bundle.crt    Fedora, Red Hat
//   /etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt  Debian, Ubuntu, Alpine
//   /etc/ssl/cert.pem                   macOS, LibreSSL, Alpine
//
// `$SSL_CERT_DIR` and per-user stores are not read. A bundle that cannot be
// read, or holds no usable root, is `TrustStore`, reported before any
// connection is made. A root that does not parse is skipped (it can only
// narrow what is trusted).
//
// **There is no way to skip verification**, and none will be added: every
// connection checks a pin or a chain, the CertificateVerify signature over the
// transcript, and the server's Finished MAC, or it does not exist. There is
// no insecure `Trust` variant, no option, no environment variable.
//
// Server certificates may be RSA, ECDSA (P-256, P-384) or Ed25519 (RFC 8410),
// and so may the signatures in the chain above them. A host that is an IP
// literal is matched against the certificate's iPAddress entries only (never
// a dNSName), and sends no server_name (RFC 6066 §3).
//
// Not done: CRLs (and fetching anything: see "revocation" below), name
// constraints, and a public suffix list (`*.co.uk` is accepted as a wildcard).
//
// --- revocation: stapled OCSP only -------------------------------------------
//
// The client sends `status_request` (RFC 6066 §8: type ocsp, no responder IDs,
// no request extensions) and checks the BasicOCSPResponse a server staples for
// its leaf: in TLS 1.3 the `status_request` extension of the leaf's
// CertificateEntry, in TLS 1.2 the CertificateStatus message (type 22) that
// follows the Certificate when the ServerHello promised one with an empty
// `status_request`. `lib/ocsp.m31` does the checking: signature (the issuer's,
// or a delegated id-kp-OCSPSigning responder's certificate carried in the
// response), CertID, thisUpdate/nextUpdate against the client's clock (five
// minutes of skew; a response with no nextUpdate is stale), status, and no
// unknown critical extension. Its header lists exactly what it accepts.
//
// The policy, which is the whole of it:
//
//   a staple that checks out and says good     accepted
//   a staple that says revoked                 `CertificateRevoked`, alert 44
//   a staple that is malformed, unsigned by
//     the issuer or an authorised responder,
//     for another certificate, expired or not
//     yet valid, "unknown", signed with SHA-1,
//     or has an unknown critical extension     `BadStapledResponse`, alert 42:
//                                              **fail closed**
//   a staple whose TLS framing is malformed
//     (duplicate, trailing octets, a status
//     type other than ocsp, ...)               `Malformed`, alert 50
//   a status message that was not promised, or
//     is not directly after the Certificate    `UnexpectedMessage`, alert 10
//   no staple at all (also an OCSPResponse
//     whose status is tryLater, unauthorized,
//     ... which carries no answer)             **soft-fail**: accepted -- unless
//                                              the leaf has the TLS Feature
//                                              extension with status_request
//                                              (RFC 7633 "must-staple"), or the
//                                              trust is `require_stapled(...)`:
//                                              then `StapleRequired`, alert 42
//
// A server can strip a staple, so soft-fail without must-staple buys a
// network attacker who can also present a revoked certificate nothing; it
// is the default because most servers do not staple and a client that fails
// on every one of them is unusable. `tls.require_stapled(trust)` is for a
// client that knows its server staples: it turns "no staple" into an error
// for every certificate. A must-staple certificate is hard-fail always, with
// no option to turn that off.
//
// Only the leaf is checked, against the certificate above it in the path.
// A pinned key (`PinnedKey`) skips the whole check, as it skips everything
// about the certificate; `require_stapled` over a pin is a trap, not a silent
// no-op. The client never fetches: no OCSP responder URL is contacted, no CRL
// is downloaded -- **CRL checking and OCSP fetching are out of scope** --, and
// no nonce is sent, so a staple can be replayed until its nextUpdate (that is
// what stapling is). A resumed session carries no new staple, but its
// lifetime is capped by the nextUpdate of the staple it was made under (as it
// is by the earliest certificate `not_after` and by seven days): it cannot
// outlive the freshness of the evidence for the certificate. `Conn.stapled()`
// says whether a staple was checked on this handshake.
//
// --- what is offered, and so what is accepted ---------------------------------
//
//   versions             TLS 1.3 and TLS 1.2, via supported_versions. The
//                        client never offers 1.1 or below, and a server that
//                        answers with them is refused. It falls back to 1.2
//                        only when the server itself picks 1.2 in its
//                        ServerHello; a 1.3-capable server that answers 1.2 is
//                        caught by the downgrade sentinel in its random
//   cipher suites        TLS_CHACHA20_POLY1305_SHA256, TLS_AES_128_GCM_SHA256 and
//                        TLS_AES_256_GCM_SHA384 for 1.3, offered in that order,
//                        then for 1.2 ECDHE_ECDSA/ECDHE_RSA with
//                        CHACHA20_POLY1305 (0xCCA9, 0xCCA8), AES_128_GCM
//                        (0xC02B, 0xC02F) and AES_256_GCM (0xC02C, 0xC030).
//                        The server picks. ChaCha20 leads because the AES-GCM
//                        in lib/aes.m31 is constant-time at the source level
//                        and so about twelve times slower (see its header).
//                        No CBC, no RC4, no static RSA key exchange, no
//                        compression, no renegotiation
//   key exchange         x25519 and secp256r1 are advertised; the 1.3
//                        key_share is x25519 only, so a 1.3 server that
//                        insists on P-256 needs a HelloRetryRequest, which is
//                        refused. In 1.2 the server picks either group from
//                        supported_groups. One fresh key per connection
//   signatures           ecdsa_secp256r1_sha256, ecdsa_secp384r1_sha384,
//                        rsa_pss_rsae_sha256/384/512 for the handshake
//                        signature (CertificateVerify); the same plus RSA
//                        PKCS#1 v1.5 with SHA-256/384/512 for the
//                        certificates (`signature_algorithms_cert`). TLS 1.2
//                        additionally accepts rsa_pkcs1_sha256/384/512 on the
//                        ServerKeyExchange; in 1.2 the ECDSA scheme names only
//                        the hash and the leaf key gives the curve
//   extensions           server_name (not for an IP address), status_request
//                        (OCSP, below), supported_groups
//                        (which RFC 8446 §4.2.7 requires beside a key_share),
//                        signature_algorithms, signature_algorithms_cert,
//                        supported_versions, key_share, and for 1.2
//                        ec_point_formats (uncompressed only),
//                        extended_master_secret and renegotiation_info
//
// `psk_key_exchange_modes` (psk_dhe_ke only) and, when the cache holds a
// session, `pre_shared_key` are added for resumption (below). There is no ALPN
// and **never any early data**. An
// extension the client did not offer, in ServerHello or EncryptedExtensions,
// is refused (RFC 8446 §4.2).
//
// A server that answers with HelloRetryRequest, with a ServerHello for TLS 1.1
// or older, or with RFC 8446 §4.1.3's downgrade sentinel in its random, is
// told no, each with an error of its own.
//
// --- TLS 1.2: the rules ---------------------------------------------------------
//
// `extended_master_secret` (RFC 7627) is **required**: a 1.2 server without it
// is refused with alert 40 (`MissingExtendedMasterSecret`), because without it
// the master secret does not bind the handshake and the triple-handshake
// attack applies. This turns away servers that are still on a pre-2015
// OpenSSL, such as the badssl.com test hosts. `renegotiation_info` (RFC 5746)
// may be absent from the ServerHello, but if present it must be exactly the
// empty first-handshake value. Renegotiation is never accepted: a HelloRequest
// after the handshake is answered with the warning alert `no_renegotiation`
// and the connection goes on; any other handshake message after the Finished
// is `UnexpectedMessage`. Every alert except `close_notify` is fatal, warnings
// included, as in 1.3. The explicit nonce of an AES-GCM record is taken from
// the record (RFC 5288 allows it), not forced to equal the sequence number.
// A zero-length handshake record is refused.
//
// `secp384r1` is not advertised, so an OpenSSL server whose only certificate
// is a P-384 key refuses us; a P-384 leaf is accepted (and tested) when the
// server has some other group to agree on. The ECDH for secp256r1 uses
// `lib/ecdsa.m31`'s non-constant-time arithmetic, which is acceptable only
// because the scalar is ephemeral and used once (see that header).
//
// --- the connection after the handshake ----------------------------------------
//
// **A connection that ends without the server's `close_notify` is an error**,
// `Truncated`, never a quiet end of data: otherwise whoever sits on the wire
// can cut a response short and it reads as complete. `read_all` returns only
// for a `close_notify`. A `KeyUpdate` is followed (and answered, when asked),
// and a `NewSessionTicket` is filed in the session cache (below).
//
// --- client certificates (TLS 1.3 only) -------------------------------------------
//
//   tls.connect_identified(host, port, trust, identity, cache)       and
//   tls.connect_over_identified(socket, host, trust, identity, cache), `_at` forms
//   clientcert.load(chain_pem, key_pem) / clientcert.load_files(chain_path, key_path)
//
// An `Identity` (`lib/clientcert.m31`) is a chain, leaf first, and the key of
// the leaf: Ed25519 or ECDSA P-256. When the server sends a CertificateRequest
// after its EncryptedExtensions (RFC 8446 §4.3.2), the client answers with
// Certificate (the chain, empty request context), CertificateVerify (signed
// by `signingkey`, which is `ecdsasign` or `ed25519`: this file has no
// signer of its own) and a Finished that covers both. The certificate_authorities
// extension is parsed and ignored; the chain goes whatever it says.
//
// * Without an identity, a CertificateRequest ends the handshake with
//   `ClientCertificateRequested` (alert handshake_failure). The client never
//   sends an empty Certificate to get past a request, even an optional one:
//   a program that wants to talk to such servers anonymously should not
//   present itself as having failed to authenticate.
// * With an identity whose key signs none of the schemes the server lists in
//   signature_algorithms, the handshake ends with `ClientCertificateUnusable`
//   (alert handshake_failure), again without sending an empty Certificate.
// * A request with a non-empty context, without signature_algorithms, with
//   it twice, with trailing or truncated bytes, or in any place but between
//   EncryptedExtensions and the server's Certificate is refused with the
//   alert for that kind of fault, before anything is signed.
// * A server that sends no CertificateRequest gets nothing: the identity is
//   used only when it is asked for.
// * Sessions are bound to the identity's fingerprint as well as to the host
//   and trust (below): a session earned with one certificate is never offered
//   by a connection with another, or with none.
// * TLS 1.2: not supported. A 1.2 CertificateRequest also gives
//   `ClientCertificateRequested`. Whether the server accepts the certificate is
//   the server's business; a rejection arrives as its alert (`peer_alert`).
//
// What is and is not constant time is in the header of `lib/clientcert.m31`
// (the signers are written constant-time-style at source level, unaudited at
// machine level; the key is not wiped from memory).
//
// --- session resumption (TLS 1.3 only) -------------------------------------------
//
//   tls.connect_cached(host, port, trust, cache)          and the `_at`, `_over_cached`,
//   tls.connect_over_cached_at(socket, host, trust, cache, now)    forms
//   tlsresume.cache(capacity)        an in-process `SessionCache` (0 = off)
//   c.resumed()                      -> bool   did this connection resume?
//
// `connect` and friends keep no sessions and ask for no tickets. With a cache
// the client offers `psk_key_exchange_modes = [psk_dhe_ke]` and files each
// NewSessionTicket as a session; the next connection to the same host takes
// the newest unexpired one and offers it as a `pre_shared_key` with its binder.
// A fresh (EC)DHE share is always sent and used (`psk_ke`, a PSK alone, is
// not offered), so a resumed connection keeps forward secrecy.
//
// * A session is bound to the exact `host` (the SNI) **and** the exact trust
//   configuration (the pin's octets, or `SystemRoots`, or the `CaFile` path)
//   of the connection that earned it, and is offered to no other.
// * A full handshake validates the certificate chain as always. Only a
//   handshake in which the server selected the PSK skips the Certificate and
//   CertificateVerify, as RFC 8446 requires; any other Certificate in such a
//   handshake is refused. The server must pick identity 0, the offered suite,
//   and send a key_share.
// * A session ends at the earliest of: the ticket lifetime, 7 days after the
//   handshake that earned it, and the `not_after` of every certificate that
//   server presented (not for a pinned key, which has no chain). A resumed
//   session earns no further trust: tickets from a resumed connection expire
//   with the original full handshake's certificates, not later.
// * A ticket is used at most once (taken from the cache when offered), a
//   ticket with lifetime 0 is not stored, and a malformed NewSessionTicket
//   fails the connection with decode_error.
// * **0-RTT is never offered**: no early_data extension is sent, whatever
//   `max_early_data_size` the ticket carried.
// * Tickets are filed while the connection is being read: a connection never
//   read after the handshake files none.
// * A server that does not recognise the ticket does a full handshake and
//   the client validates it; the failed ticket is already spent.
//
// `io.Stream` fixes the error type at `io.Error`, and `Error` below is richer
// than that, so a failed call answers `Other(errno)` -- 5 (EIO) for a failed
// underlying stream, 104 (ECONNRESET) for a stream that ended without a
// `close_notify`, 9 (EBADF) for a connection already closed, 71 (EPROTO) for
// everything the peer got wrong -- and `last_error()` has the exact reason.
// A failure ends the connection: every later call repeats it. There are no
// timeouts to retry after, since a read that gave up half way through a
// record could not be resumed.
//
// `read_all` and `read_until` hand back what they had collected to the
// connection when a failure comes, as `net.Conn`'s do, but the failure ends
// the connection so only `read` of what was buffered still works.
//
// **The handshake can be bounded as a whole.** `net.Conn`'s read timeout limits
// one read, so a server dripping a byte at a time inside it can hold a
// handshake open for as long as it likes. `connect_over_deadline` adds a total
// (`deadlinestream`): once it passes the handshake fails with `Error.Io`. It
// reads the wall clock, as the language has no monotonic clock. The plain
// `connect` forms keep the per-read behaviour of the socket they were given.
//
// **A `Conn` sends `close_notify` and closes the socket when its last
// reference goes**, as a `net.Conn` closes itself.

import consttime;
import deadlinestream;
import io;
import net;
import random;
import sha256;
import sha512;
import x25519;
import x509;
import x509chain;
import ecdsa;
import ed25519;
import rsa;
import hexcodec;
import date;
import os;
import tls13schedule;
import tls13record;
import tls12;
import tlsresume;
import clientcert;
import signingkey;
import ocsp;

const int HANDSHAKE_CLIENT_HELLO = 1;
const int HANDSHAKE_SERVER_HELLO = 2;
const int HANDSHAKE_ENCRYPTED_EXTENSIONS = 8;
const int HANDSHAKE_CERTIFICATE = 11;
const int HANDSHAKE_SERVER_KEY_EXCHANGE = 12;
const int HANDSHAKE_CERTIFICATE_REQUEST = 13;
const int HANDSHAKE_CERTIFICATE_STATUS = 22;
const int HANDSHAKE_SERVER_HELLO_DONE = 14;
const int HANDSHAKE_CERTIFICATE_VERIFY = 15;
const int HANDSHAKE_FINISHED = 20;
const int HANDSHAKE_CLIENT_KEY_EXCHANGE = 16;
const int GROUP_X25519 = 0x001D;
const int GROUP_SECP256R1 = 0x0017;
const int CURVE_TYPE_NAMED = 3;
const int SCHEME_ECDSA_P256_SHA256 = 0x0403;
const int SCHEME_ECDSA_P384_SHA384 = 0x0503;
const int SCHEME_ED25519 = 0x0807;
const int SCHEME_RSA_PSS_RSAE_SHA256 = 0x0804;
const int SCHEME_RSA_PSS_RSAE_SHA384 = 0x0805;
const int SCHEME_RSA_PSS_RSAE_SHA512 = 0x0806;
const int SCHEME_RSA_PKCS1_SHA256 = 0x0401;
const int SCHEME_RSA_PKCS1_SHA384 = 0x0501;
const int SCHEME_RSA_PKCS1_SHA512 = 0x0601;
const int VERSION_TLS12 = 0x0303;
const int VERSION_TLS13 = 0x0304;
const int EXTENSION_SERVER_NAME = 0;
const int EXTENSION_STATUS_REQUEST = 5;
const int STATUS_TYPE_OCSP = 1;
const int EXTENSION_EC_POINT_FORMATS = 11;
const int EXTENSION_SUPPORTED_GROUPS = 10;
const int EXTENSION_EXTENDED_MASTER_SECRET = 23;
const int EXTENSION_RENEGOTIATION_INFO = 0xFF01;
const int EXTENSION_SIGNATURE_ALGORITHMS = 13;
const int EXTENSION_SIGNATURE_ALGORITHMS_CERT = 50;
const int EXTENSION_SUPPORTED_VERSIONS = 43;
const int EXTENSION_KEY_SHARE = 51;
const int EXTENSION_PRE_SHARED_KEY = 41;
const int EXTENSION_PSK_KEY_EXCHANGE_MODES = 45;
const int PSK_DHE_KE = 1;
const int ALERT_HANDSHAKE_FAILURE = 40;
const int ALERT_BAD_CERTIFICATE = 42;
const int ALERT_CERTIFICATE_REVOKED = 44;
const int ALERT_CERTIFICATE_EXPIRED = 45;
const int ALERT_ILLEGAL_PARAMETER = 47;
const int ALERT_UNKNOWN_CA = 48;
const int ALERT_DECODE_ERROR = 50;
const int ALERT_DECRYPT_ERROR = 51;
const int ALERT_PROTOCOL_VERSION = 70;
const int ALERT_UNSUPPORTED_EXTENSION = 110;
const int ERRNO_BAD_DESCRIPTOR = 9;
const int ERRNO_IO = 5;
const int ERRNO_PROTOCOL = 71;
const int ERRNO_RESET = 104;
const int RANDOM_SIZE = 32;
const int SESSION_ID_SIZE = 32;
const int X25519_SIZE = 32;
const int P256_POINT_SIZE = 65;

/// Whose key the server must prove it holds.
pub enum Trust {
    /// The SHA-256 of the leaf certificate's DER SubjectPublicKeyInfo.
    PinnedKey(bytes);
    /// A chain to a root in the operating system's CA bundle.
    SystemRoots;
    /// A chain to a root in this PEM file.
    CaFile(str);
    /// The same trust, and in addition every server certificate must come
    /// with a valid stapled OCSP response (see "revocation" in the header).
    /// Made by `require_stapled`; not meaningful over a pin.
    RequireStapled(Trust);
}

/// `trust` with a stapled OCSP response required of every server, whether
/// or not its certificate says must-staple. A pin **traps**: a pinned key is
/// not examined for revocation, so the demand could never be met.
pub Trust require_stapled(Trust trust) {
    match (trust) {
        case PinnedKey(bytes pinned): {
            trap("tls: require_stapled over a pinned key: a pin is not checked for revocation");
        }
        case SystemRoots: {
            return Trust.RequireStapled(trust);
        }
        case CaFile(str path): {
            return Trust.RequireStapled(trust);
        }
        case RequireStapled(Trust inner): {
            return trust;
        }
    }
}

/// A key pin from its 32 octets. Anything else **traps**: a pin of the wrong
/// size would refuse every server, and silently.
pub Trust pinned_key(bytes key_hash) {
    if (key_hash.size() != 32) {
        trap("tls: a key pin is the 32 octets of a SHA-256 hash");
    }
    return Trust.PinnedKey(key_hash);
}

/// A key pin from 64 hex digits, `None` if `text` is anything else.
pub Option<Trust> pinned_key_hex(str text) {
    match (hexcodec.decode(text)) {
        case Err(hexcodec.Error problem): {
            return Option<Trust>.None;
        }
        case Ok(bytes key_hash): {
            if (key_hash.size() != 32) {
                return Option<Trust>.None;
            }
            return Option<Trust>.Some(Trust.PinnedKey(key_hash));
        }
    }
}

/// Why a connection could not be made, or stopped. Payload-free
/// (`docs/errors-decision.md` §4); the alert a peer sent is
/// `Conn.peer_alert()`.
pub enum Error {
    /// The TCP connection could not be made.
    Connect;
    /// The stream failed under the connection: a reset, a timeout.
    Io;
    /// The stream ended without a `close_notify`.
    Truncated;
    /// The server sent a fatal alert.
    PeerAlert;
    /// The server's records broke a rule of the record layer: a version, a
    /// size, a sequence number.
    Record;
    /// A record's authentication tag did not verify.
    BadRecordMac;
    /// The connection is closed, by this side or by an earlier failure.
    Closed;
    /// The server speaks TLS 1.1 or older, which this client does not.
    UnsupportedVersion;
    /// The server's random carries the TLS 1.3 downgrade sentinel.
    Downgrade;
    /// The server answered with HelloRetryRequest, which this client, having
    /// offered the one group it will use, cannot follow.
    RetryRequested;
    /// A ServerHello field that is not what was offered.
    IllegalParameter;
    /// An extension the client never offered.
    UnsupportedExtension;
    /// A message that is not well formed.
    Malformed;
    /// A message that this point in the handshake does not allow.
    UnexpectedMessage;
    /// The server asked for a client certificate and this connection has no
    /// identity (or is TLS 1.2, where client certificates are not supported).
    ClientCertificateRequested;
    /// The server asked for a client certificate, but none of the signature
    /// schemes it accepts is one the caller's key signs with.
    ClientCertificateUnusable;
    /// The server's certificate is missing or not one this client can read.
    BadCertificate;
    /// The server's key is not the pinned one.
    UntrustedKey;
    /// The CA bundle could not be read, or holds no usable root.
    TrustStore;
    /// The certificate chain does not lead to a trusted root.
    UnknownAuthority;
    /// A certificate in the chain has expired.
    CertificateExpired;
    /// A certificate in the chain is not yet valid.
    CertificateNotYetValid;
    /// The certificate is not valid for the host that was asked for.
    HostnameMismatch;
    /// The chain breaks a rule: a signature, a CA constraint, a path length,
    /// a purpose.
    InvalidChain;
    /// The chain is signed with an algorithm or key too weak, or one this
    /// client does not implement.
    WeakCertificate;
    /// A CertificateVerify scheme that was not offered, or one that does not
    /// fit the certificate's key.
    UnsupportedSignature;
    /// The CertificateVerify signature does not verify: the server does not
    /// hold the key.
    BadSignature;
    /// The server's Finished does not verify: the handshake was altered.
    BadFinished;
    /// A TLS 1.2 server that does not do extended_master_secret (RFC 7627).
    MissingExtendedMasterSecret;
    /// A TLS 1.2 renegotiation_info that is not the empty one of a first
    /// handshake (RFC 5746).
    BadRenegotiationInfo;
    /// The server's stapled OCSP response says the certificate is revoked.
    CertificateRevoked;
    /// The server stapled an OCSP response that cannot be believed: it is
    /// malformed, unsigned by the issuer or an authorised responder, stale,
    /// for another certificate, or says "unknown".
    BadStapledResponse;
    /// A stapled OCSP response was required, by the certificate's
    /// must-staple extension or by `require_stapled`, and the server sent
    /// none (or one that carries no answer).
    StapleRequired;
}

pub str Error.to_str() {
    match (this) {
        case Connect: {
            return "could not connect";
        }
        case Io: {
            return "the underlying connection failed";
        }
        case Truncated: {
            return "the connection ended without a close_notify";
        }
        case PeerAlert: {
            return "the server sent a fatal alert";
        }
        case Record: {
            return "the server broke a rule of the TLS record layer";
        }
        case BadRecordMac: {
            return "a record's authentication tag did not verify";
        }
        case Closed: {
            return "the connection is closed";
        }
        case UnsupportedVersion: {
            return "the server does not speak TLS 1.2 or 1.3";
        }
        case Downgrade: {
            return "the server's random carries the TLS downgrade sentinel";
        }
        case RetryRequested: {
            return "the server sent a HelloRetryRequest, which this client does not support";
        }
        case IllegalParameter: {
            return "the server's hello is not what was offered";
        }
        case UnsupportedExtension: {
            return "the server used an extension that was not offered";
        }
        case Malformed: {
            return "a handshake message is malformed";
        }
        case UnexpectedMessage: {
            return "a handshake message arrived where it is not allowed";
        }
        case ClientCertificateRequested: {
            return "the server asked for a client certificate";
        }
        case ClientCertificateUnusable: {
            return "the server accepts no signature scheme the client certificate's key signs with";
        }
        case BadCertificate: {
            return "the server's certificate is missing or unreadable";
        }
        case UntrustedKey: {
            return "the server's key is not the pinned key";
        }
        case TrustStore: {
            return "the trusted root certificates could not be loaded";
        }
        case UnknownAuthority: {
            return "the server's certificate was not issued by a trusted authority";
        }
        case CertificateExpired: {
            return "a certificate in the server's chain has expired";
        }
        case CertificateNotYetValid: {
            return "a certificate in the server's chain is not yet valid";
        }
        case HostnameMismatch: {
            return "the server's certificate is not valid for this host name";
        }
        case InvalidChain: {
            return "the server's certificate chain is not valid";
        }
        case WeakCertificate: {
            return "the server's certificate chain uses a weak or unsupported algorithm";
        }
        case UnsupportedSignature: {
            return "the server signed with a scheme that was not offered or does not fit its key";
        }
        case BadSignature: {
            return "the server's handshake signature is wrong";
        }
        case BadFinished: {
            return "the server's Finished does not verify";
        }
        case MissingExtendedMasterSecret: {
            return "the TLS 1.2 server does not support extended_master_secret";
        }
        case BadRenegotiationInfo: {
            return "the server's renegotiation_info is not that of a first handshake";
        }
        case CertificateRevoked: {
            return "the server's certificate is revoked";
        }
        case BadStapledResponse: {
            return "the server's stapled OCSP response cannot be trusted";
        }
        case StapleRequired: {
            return "a stapled OCSP response was required and the server sent none";
        }
    }
}

// The alert that tells the server why, or -1 where the record layer has
// already sent one or there is nobody to send to.
int alert_for(Error error) {
    match (error) {
        case Connect: {
            return -1;
        }
        case Io: {
            return -1;
        }
        case Truncated: {
            return -1;
        }
        case PeerAlert: {
            return -1;
        }
        case Record: {
            return -1;
        }
        case BadRecordMac: {
            return -1;
        }
        case Closed: {
            return -1;
        }
        case UnsupportedVersion: {
            return ALERT_PROTOCOL_VERSION;
        }
        case Downgrade: {
            return ALERT_ILLEGAL_PARAMETER;
        }
        case RetryRequested: {
            return ALERT_HANDSHAKE_FAILURE;
        }
        case IllegalParameter: {
            return ALERT_ILLEGAL_PARAMETER;
        }
        case UnsupportedExtension: {
            return ALERT_UNSUPPORTED_EXTENSION;
        }
        case Malformed: {
            return ALERT_DECODE_ERROR;
        }
        case UnexpectedMessage: {
            return tls13record.ALERT_UNEXPECTED_MESSAGE;
        }
        case ClientCertificateRequested: {
            return ALERT_HANDSHAKE_FAILURE;
        }
        case ClientCertificateUnusable: {
            return ALERT_HANDSHAKE_FAILURE;
        }
        case BadCertificate: {
            return ALERT_BAD_CERTIFICATE;
        }
        case UntrustedKey: {
            return ALERT_BAD_CERTIFICATE;
        }
        case TrustStore: {
            return -1;
        }
        case UnknownAuthority: {
            return ALERT_UNKNOWN_CA;
        }
        case CertificateExpired: {
            return ALERT_CERTIFICATE_EXPIRED;
        }
        case CertificateNotYetValid: {
            return ALERT_BAD_CERTIFICATE;
        }
        case HostnameMismatch: {
            return ALERT_BAD_CERTIFICATE;
        }
        case InvalidChain: {
            return ALERT_BAD_CERTIFICATE;
        }
        case WeakCertificate: {
            return ALERT_BAD_CERTIFICATE;
        }
        case UnsupportedSignature: {
            return ALERT_ILLEGAL_PARAMETER;
        }
        case BadSignature: {
            return ALERT_DECRYPT_ERROR;
        }
        case BadFinished: {
            return ALERT_DECRYPT_ERROR;
        }
        case MissingExtendedMasterSecret: {
            return ALERT_HANDSHAKE_FAILURE;
        }
        case BadRenegotiationInfo: {
            return ALERT_HANDSHAKE_FAILURE;
        }
        case CertificateRevoked: {
            return ALERT_CERTIFICATE_REVOKED;
        }
        case BadStapledResponse: {
            return ALERT_BAD_CERTIFICATE;
        }
        case StapleRequired: {
            return ALERT_BAD_CERTIFICATE;
        }
    }
}

// The errno an `io.Error` carries for `error`.
int errno_of(Error error) {
    match (error) {
        case Io: {
            return ERRNO_IO;
        }
        case Truncated: {
            return ERRNO_RESET;
        }
        case Closed: {
            return ERRNO_BAD_DESCRIPTOR;
        }
        case Connect: {
            return ERRNO_PROTOCOL;
        }
        case PeerAlert: {
            return ERRNO_PROTOCOL;
        }
        case Record: {
            return ERRNO_PROTOCOL;
        }
        case BadRecordMac: {
            return ERRNO_PROTOCOL;
        }
        case UnsupportedVersion: {
            return ERRNO_PROTOCOL;
        }
        case Downgrade: {
            return ERRNO_PROTOCOL;
        }
        case RetryRequested: {
            return ERRNO_PROTOCOL;
        }
        case IllegalParameter: {
            return ERRNO_PROTOCOL;
        }
        case UnsupportedExtension: {
            return ERRNO_PROTOCOL;
        }
        case Malformed: {
            return ERRNO_PROTOCOL;
        }
        case UnexpectedMessage: {
            return ERRNO_PROTOCOL;
        }
        case ClientCertificateRequested: {
            return ERRNO_PROTOCOL;
        }
        case ClientCertificateUnusable: {
            return ERRNO_PROTOCOL;
        }
        case BadCertificate: {
            return ERRNO_PROTOCOL;
        }
        case UntrustedKey: {
            return ERRNO_PROTOCOL;
        }
        case TrustStore: {
            return ERRNO_PROTOCOL;
        }
        case UnknownAuthority: {
            return ERRNO_PROTOCOL;
        }
        case CertificateExpired: {
            return ERRNO_PROTOCOL;
        }
        case CertificateNotYetValid: {
            return ERRNO_PROTOCOL;
        }
        case HostnameMismatch: {
            return ERRNO_PROTOCOL;
        }
        case InvalidChain: {
            return ERRNO_PROTOCOL;
        }
        case WeakCertificate: {
            return ERRNO_PROTOCOL;
        }
        case UnsupportedSignature: {
            return ERRNO_PROTOCOL;
        }
        case BadSignature: {
            return ERRNO_PROTOCOL;
        }
        case BadFinished: {
            return ERRNO_PROTOCOL;
        }
        case MissingExtendedMasterSecret: {
            return ERRNO_PROTOCOL;
        }
        case BadRenegotiationInfo: {
            return ERRNO_PROTOCOL;
        }
        case CertificateRevoked: {
            return ERRNO_PROTOCOL;
        }
        case BadStapledResponse: {
            return ERRNO_PROTOCOL;
        }
        case StapleRequired: {
            return ERRNO_PROTOCOL;
        }
    }
}

Error error_of_record(tls13record.Error error) {
    match (error) {
        case Io: {
            return Error.Io;
        }
        case Truncated: {
            return Error.Truncated;
        }
        case PeerAlert: {
            return Error.PeerAlert;
        }
        case BadRecordMac: {
            return Error.BadRecordMac;
        }
        case UnexpectedMessage: {
            return Error.UnexpectedMessage;
        }
        case DecodeError: {
            return Error.Malformed;
        }
        case Closed: {
            return Error.Closed;
        }
        case BadVersion: {
            return Error.Record;
        }
        case RecordOverflow: {
            return Error.Record;
        }
        case HandshakeTooLarge: {
            return Error.Record;
        }
        case SequenceExhausted: {
            return Error.Record;
        }
    }
}

// --- byte-level helpers ---------------------------------------------------------

void put_u16(bytes out, int value) {
    out.push((value >> 8) & 0xFF);
    out.push(value & 0xFF);
}

void put_u24(bytes out, int value) {
    out.push((value >> 16) & 0xFF);
    out.push((value >> 8) & 0xFF);
    out.push(value & 0xFF);
}

// `content` behind its 16-bit length.
void put_u32(bytes out, int value) {
    put_u16(out, (value >> 16) & 0xFFFF);
    put_u16(out, value & 0xFFFF);
}

bytes with_u16_length(bytes content) {
    bytes out = [];
    put_u16(out, content.size());
    out.extend(content);
    return out;
}

bytes extension(int kind, bytes data) {
    bytes out = [];
    put_u16(out, kind);
    out.extend(with_u16_length(data));
    return out;
}

bool all_zero(bytes data) {
    int accumulated = 0;
    for (int index in 0 .. data.size()) {
        accumulated = accumulated | data[index];
    }
    return accumulated == 0;
}

// What a received message holds after its four-octet header.
bytes message_body(bytes message) {
    return message.substr(4, message.size());
}

// A cursor over a received message's body, every read of which can find the
// message too short.
type Reader {
    bytes data;
    int at = 0;
}

int Reader.remaining() {
    return data.size() - at;
}

Result<int, Error> Reader.u8() {
    if (remaining() < 1) {
        return Result<int, Error>.Err(Error.Malformed);
    }
    int value = data[at];
    at = at + 1;
    return Result<int, Error>.Ok(value);
}

Result<int, Error> Reader.u16() {
    if (remaining() < 2) {
        return Result<int, Error>.Err(Error.Malformed);
    }
    int value = data[at] * 256 + data[at + 1];
    at = at + 2;
    return Result<int, Error>.Ok(value);
}

Result<bytes, Error> Reader.take(int count) {
    if (count < 0 || remaining() < count) {
        return Result<bytes, Error>.Err(Error.Malformed);
    }
    bytes out = data.substr(at, at + count);
    at = at + count;
    return Result<bytes, Error>.Ok(out);
}

Result<bytes, Error> Reader.vector8() {
    int count = u8()?;
    return take(count);
}

Result<bytes, Error> Reader.vector16() {
    int count = u16()?;
    return take(count);
}

Result<bytes, Error> Reader.vector24() {
    if (remaining() < 3) {
        return Result<bytes, Error>.Err(Error.Malformed);
    }
    int count = data[at] * 65536 + data[at + 1] * 256 + data[at + 2];
    at = at + 3;
    return take(count);
}

// --- ClientHello ------------------------------------------------------------------

bool is_ip_literal(str host) {
    match (net.parse_ip(host)) {
        case Some(net.Ip address): {
            return true;
        }
        case None: {
            return false;
        }
    }
}

// RFC 6066 §3: a list of one host_name entry. Never sent for an IP address.
bytes server_name_data(str host) {
    bytes name = host.to_bytes();
    bytes entry = [0];
    entry.extend(with_u16_length(name));
    return with_u16_length(entry);
}

// The TLS 1.3 suites in the ClientHello, most preferred first.
List<tls13schedule.Suite> offered_suites() {
    List<tls13schedule.Suite> suites = [tls13schedule.Suite.ChaCha20Poly1305Sha256, tls13schedule.Suite.Aes128GcmSha256, tls13schedule.Suite.Aes256GcmSha384];
    return suites;
}

// `offer_tickets` adds psk_key_exchange_modes (psk_dhe_ke only), which is what
// lets a server send NewSessionTickets at all. `resumption`, when there, adds
// the pre_shared_key extension for that session -- last, as RFC 8446 §4.2.11
// requires, its binder computed over the message up to the binders. No
// early_data extension is ever sent: there is no 0-RTT.
bytes client_hello(str host, bytes client_random, bytes session_id, bytes public_key, bool offer_tickets, Option<tlsresume.Session> resumption, int now) {
    bytes body = [];
    put_u16(body, VERSION_TLS12);
    body.extend(client_random);
    body.push(session_id.size());
    body.extend(session_id);
    List<tls13schedule.Suite> suites = offered_suites();
    List<tls12.Suite> legacy_suites = tls12.all_suites();
    put_u16(body, 2 * suites.size() + 2 * legacy_suites.size());
    for (tls13schedule.Suite offered in suites) {
        put_u16(body, offered.code());
    }
    for (tls12.Suite legacy_offered in legacy_suites) {
        put_u16(body, legacy_offered.code());
    }
    body.push(1);
    body.push(0);
    bytes extensions = [];
    if (!is_ip_literal(host)) {
        extensions.extend(extension(EXTENSION_SERVER_NAME, server_name_data(host)));
    }
    extensions.extend(extension(EXTENSION_STATUS_REQUEST, [STATUS_TYPE_OCSP, 0, 0, 0, 0]));
    bytes groups = [];
    put_u16(groups, GROUP_X25519);
    put_u16(groups, GROUP_SECP256R1);
    extensions.extend(extension(EXTENSION_SUPPORTED_GROUPS, with_u16_length(groups)));
    extensions.extend(extension(EXTENSION_EC_POINT_FORMATS, [1, 0]));
    bytes schemes = [];
    put_u16(schemes, SCHEME_ECDSA_P256_SHA256);
    put_u16(schemes, SCHEME_ECDSA_P384_SHA384);
    put_u16(schemes, SCHEME_ED25519);
    put_u16(schemes, SCHEME_RSA_PSS_RSAE_SHA256);
    put_u16(schemes, SCHEME_RSA_PSS_RSAE_SHA384);
    put_u16(schemes, SCHEME_RSA_PSS_RSAE_SHA512);
    put_u16(schemes, SCHEME_RSA_PKCS1_SHA256);
    put_u16(schemes, SCHEME_RSA_PKCS1_SHA384);
    put_u16(schemes, SCHEME_RSA_PKCS1_SHA512);
    extensions.extend(extension(EXTENSION_SIGNATURE_ALGORITHMS, with_u16_length(schemes)));
    bytes certificate_schemes = [];
    put_u16(certificate_schemes, SCHEME_ECDSA_P256_SHA256);
    put_u16(certificate_schemes, SCHEME_ECDSA_P384_SHA384);
    put_u16(certificate_schemes, SCHEME_ED25519);
    put_u16(certificate_schemes, SCHEME_RSA_PSS_RSAE_SHA256);
    put_u16(certificate_schemes, SCHEME_RSA_PSS_RSAE_SHA384);
    put_u16(certificate_schemes, SCHEME_RSA_PSS_RSAE_SHA512);
    put_u16(certificate_schemes, SCHEME_RSA_PKCS1_SHA256);
    put_u16(certificate_schemes, SCHEME_RSA_PKCS1_SHA384);
    put_u16(certificate_schemes, SCHEME_RSA_PKCS1_SHA512);
    extensions.extend(extension(EXTENSION_SIGNATURE_ALGORITHMS_CERT, with_u16_length(certificate_schemes)));
    extensions.extend(extension(EXTENSION_EXTENDED_MASTER_SECRET, []));
    extensions.extend(extension(EXTENSION_RENEGOTIATION_INFO, [0]));
    bytes versions = [4];
    put_u16(versions, VERSION_TLS13);
    put_u16(versions, VERSION_TLS12);
    extensions.extend(extension(EXTENSION_SUPPORTED_VERSIONS, versions));
    bytes share = [];
    put_u16(share, GROUP_X25519);
    share.extend(with_u16_length(public_key));
    extensions.extend(extension(EXTENSION_KEY_SHARE, with_u16_length(share)));
    if (offer_tickets) {
        extensions.extend(extension(EXTENSION_PSK_KEY_EXCHANGE_MODES, [1, PSK_DHE_KE]));
    }
    match (resumption) {
        case Some(tlsresume.Session offered): {
            bytes identity = with_u16_length(offered.ticket);
            put_u32(identity, offered.obfuscated_age(now));
            bytes binders = [offered.suite.hash_size()];
            bytes placeholder = [0; offered.suite.hash_size()];
            binders.extend(placeholder);
            bytes preshared = with_u16_length(identity);
            preshared.extend(with_u16_length(binders));
            extensions.extend(extension(EXTENSION_PRE_SHARED_KEY, preshared));
        }
        case None: {
        }
    }
    put_u16(body, extensions.size());
    body.extend(extensions);
    bytes message = [HANDSHAKE_CLIENT_HELLO];
    put_u24(message, body.size());
    message.extend(body);
    match (resumption) {
        case Some(tlsresume.Session bound): {
            fill_binder(message, bound);
        }
        case None: {
        }
    }
    return message;
}

// RFC 8446 §4.2.11.2: the binder is the Finished-style HMAC, under the key
// derived from the PSK, of the hash of the ClientHello cut off before the
// binders list (a 2-octet length, then one binder with its 1-octet length).
// It is written over the zeros that stand in for it.
void fill_binder(bytes message, tlsresume.Session session) {
    tls13schedule.Suite suite = session.suite;
    int tail = 3 + suite.hash_size();
    bytes truncated = message.substr(0, message.size() - tail);
    bytes early = tls13schedule.early_secret(suite, session.psk);
    bytes finished = tls13schedule.finished_key(suite, tls13schedule.binder_key(suite, early, true));
    bytes binder = tls13schedule.finished_verify_data(suite, finished, tls13schedule.hash_octets(suite, truncated));
    for (int index in 0 .. binder.size()) {
        message[message.size() - binder.size() + index] = binder[index];
    }
}

// --- ServerHello ------------------------------------------------------------------

// RFC 8446 §4.1.3: a ServerHello whose random is the SHA-256 of this text is
// a HelloRetryRequest.
bytes retry_request_random() {
    return sha256.sum("HelloRetryRequest".to_bytes());
}

// RFC 8446 §4.1.3: the last eight octets of the random of a server that
// supports TLS 1.3 but is answering with an older version: "DOWNGRD" and a 1
// (TLS 1.2) or a 0 (TLS 1.1 and below).
bool has_downgrade_sentinel(bytes server_random) {
    bytes tail = server_random.substr(24, 32);
    bytes name = "DOWNGRD".to_bytes();
    if (!consttime.is_equal(tail.substr(0, 7), name)) {
        return false;
    }
    return tail[7] == 0 || tail[7] == 1;
}

// What a ServerHello settled. `legacy` says which of the two suites is the
// one the server chose; `public_key` is its x25519 key_share, TLS 1.3 only.
type ServerChoice {
    bool legacy;
    tls13schedule.Suite suite;
    tls12.Suite legacy_suite;
    bytes public_key;
    bytes server_random;
    // The server took the pre_shared_key we offered (identity 0).
    bool psk_selected;
    // TLS 1.2: the server answered our status_request, and so may send a
    // CertificateStatus message after its Certificate.
    bool status_promised;
}

// The extensions of a ServerHello as they came, each kind at most once.
type HelloExtensions {
    List<int> kinds;
    List<bytes> datas;
}

bool HelloExtensions.has(int kind) {
    return kinds.contains(kind);
}

bytes HelloExtensions.data_of(int kind) {
    for (int index in 0 .. kinds.size()) {
        if (kinds[index] == kind) {
            return datas[index];
        }
    }
    trap("tls: data_of an extension that is not there");
}

Result<HelloExtensions, Error> parse_hello_extensions(bytes extension_block) {
    Reader extensions = Reader(extension_block);
    List<int> kinds = [];
    List<bytes> datas = [];
    while (extensions.remaining() > 0) {
        int kind = extensions.u16()?;
        bytes data = extensions.vector16()?;
        if (kinds.contains(kind)) {
            return Result<HelloExtensions, Error>.Err(Error.IllegalParameter);
        }
        kinds.push(kind);
        datas.push(data);
    }
    return Result<HelloExtensions, Error>.Ok(HelloExtensions(kinds, datas));
}

// The server's choice, once every field of its ServerHello has been checked
// against what the ClientHello offered. A ServerHello is TLS 1.3's when it
// carries supported_versions = 0x0304 and TLS 1.2's when it carries none and
// says 0x0303 (RFC 8446 §4.2.1); anything else is refused.
Result<ServerChoice, Error> check_server_hello(bytes message, bytes session_id, bool sent_server_name, bool offered_psk) {
    Reader reader = Reader(message_body(message));
    int legacy_version = reader.u16()?;
    bytes server_random = reader.take(RANDOM_SIZE)?;
    if (consttime.is_equal(server_random, retry_request_random())) {
        return Result<ServerChoice, Error>.Err(Error.RetryRequested);
    }
    bytes echoed_session_id = reader.vector8()?;
    int suite_code = reader.u16()?;
    int compression = reader.u8()?;
    bytes extension_block = [];
    if (reader.remaining() > 0) {
        extension_block = reader.vector16()?;
    }
    if (reader.remaining() != 0) {
        return Result<ServerChoice, Error>.Err(Error.Malformed);
    }
    HelloExtensions found = parse_hello_extensions(extension_block)?;
    if (has_downgrade_sentinel(server_random)) {
        return Result<ServerChoice, Error>.Err(Error.Downgrade);
    }
    if (found.has(EXTENSION_SUPPORTED_VERSIONS)) {
        bytes version_data = found.data_of(EXTENSION_SUPPORTED_VERSIONS);
        if (version_data.size() != 2 || version_data[0] != 3 || version_data[1] != 4) {
            return Result<ServerChoice, Error>.Err(Error.IllegalParameter);
        }
        return check_tls13_hello(legacy_version, server_random, echoed_session_id, suite_code, compression, found, session_id, offered_psk);
    }
    if (legacy_version < VERSION_TLS12) {
        return Result<ServerChoice, Error>.Err(Error.UnsupportedVersion);
    }
    if (legacy_version != VERSION_TLS12) {
        return Result<ServerChoice, Error>.Err(Error.IllegalParameter);
    }
    return check_tls12_hello(server_random, echoed_session_id, suite_code, compression, found, session_id, sent_server_name);
}

Result<ServerChoice, Error> check_tls13_hello(int legacy_version, bytes server_random, bytes echoed_session_id, int suite_code, int compression, HelloExtensions found, bytes session_id, bool offered_psk) {
    if (legacy_version != VERSION_TLS12 || !consttime.is_equal(echoed_session_id, session_id) || compression != 0) {
        return Result<ServerChoice, Error>.Err(Error.IllegalParameter);
    }
    Option<tls13schedule.Suite> chosen = tls13schedule.suite_for_code(suite_code);
    tls13schedule.Suite suite = tls13schedule.Suite.ChaCha20Poly1305Sha256;
    match (chosen) {
        case Some(tls13schedule.Suite known): {
            suite = known;
        }
        case None: {
            return Result<ServerChoice, Error>.Err(Error.IllegalParameter);
        }
    }
    for (int kind in found.kinds) {
        bool allowed = kind == EXTENSION_KEY_SHARE || kind == EXTENSION_SUPPORTED_VERSIONS || (kind == EXTENSION_PRE_SHARED_KEY && offered_psk);
        if (!allowed) {
            return Result<ServerChoice, Error>.Err(Error.UnsupportedExtension);
        }
    }
    if (!found.has(EXTENSION_KEY_SHARE)) {
        return Result<ServerChoice, Error>.Err(Error.IllegalParameter);
    }
    bool psk_selected = found.has(EXTENSION_PRE_SHARED_KEY);
    if (psk_selected) {
        Reader selection = Reader(found.data_of(EXTENSION_PRE_SHARED_KEY));
        int identity = selection.u16()?;
        if (selection.remaining() != 0) {
            return Result<ServerChoice, Error>.Err(Error.Malformed);
        }
        if (identity != 0) {
            return Result<ServerChoice, Error>.Err(Error.IllegalParameter);
        }
    }
    Reader share = Reader(found.data_of(EXTENSION_KEY_SHARE));
    int group = share.u16()?;
    bytes public_key = share.vector16()?;
    if (share.remaining() != 0) {
        return Result<ServerChoice, Error>.Err(Error.Malformed);
    }
    if (group != GROUP_X25519 || public_key.size() != X25519_SIZE) {
        return Result<ServerChoice, Error>.Err(Error.IllegalParameter);
    }
    return Result<ServerChoice, Error>.Ok(ServerChoice(false, suite, tls12.Suite.EcdsaChaCha20Poly1305, public_key, server_random, psk_selected, false));
}

// RFC 5246 §7.4.1.3 with RFC 7627 and RFC 5746: the server picked one of the
// six suites, no compression, and no session (an echoed session id would be
// a resumption, which this client never offered -- it sent a random id only
// for TLS 1.3's sake). Of the extensions only the four that answer ours are
// allowed, each once; extended_master_secret is required and
// renegotiation_info, when there, is the empty one of a first handshake.
Result<ServerChoice, Error> check_tls12_hello(bytes server_random, bytes echoed_session_id, int suite_code, int compression, HelloExtensions found, bytes session_id, bool sent_server_name) {
    if (compression != 0 || echoed_session_id.size() > SESSION_ID_SIZE) {
        return Result<ServerChoice, Error>.Err(Error.IllegalParameter);
    }
    if (echoed_session_id.size() > 0 && consttime.is_equal(echoed_session_id, session_id)) {
        return Result<ServerChoice, Error>.Err(Error.IllegalParameter);
    }
    tls12.Suite suite = tls12.Suite.EcdsaChaCha20Poly1305;
    match (tls12.suite_for_code(suite_code)) {
        case Some(tls12.Suite known): {
            suite = known;
        }
        case None: {
            return Result<ServerChoice, Error>.Err(Error.IllegalParameter);
        }
    }
    for (int kind in found.kinds) {
        if (kind != EXTENSION_STATUS_REQUEST && kind != EXTENSION_EC_POINT_FORMATS && kind != EXTENSION_EXTENDED_MASTER_SECRET && kind != EXTENSION_RENEGOTIATION_INFO && kind != EXTENSION_SERVER_NAME) {
            return Result<ServerChoice, Error>.Err(Error.UnsupportedExtension);
        }
        if (kind == EXTENSION_SERVER_NAME && !sent_server_name) {
            return Result<ServerChoice, Error>.Err(Error.UnsupportedExtension);
        }
    }
    if (found.has(EXTENSION_SERVER_NAME) && found.data_of(EXTENSION_SERVER_NAME).size() != 0) {
        return Result<ServerChoice, Error>.Err(Error.IllegalParameter);
    }
    if (found.has(EXTENSION_STATUS_REQUEST) && found.data_of(EXTENSION_STATUS_REQUEST).size() != 0) {
        return Result<ServerChoice, Error>.Err(Error.IllegalParameter);
    }
    if (found.has(EXTENSION_EC_POINT_FORMATS)) {
        Reader formats = Reader(found.data_of(EXTENSION_EC_POINT_FORMATS));
        bytes listed = formats.vector8()?;
        bool has_uncompressed = false;
        for (int index in 0 .. listed.size()) {
            if (listed[index] == 0) {
                has_uncompressed = true;
            }
        }
        if (formats.remaining() != 0 || !has_uncompressed) {
            return Result<ServerChoice, Error>.Err(Error.IllegalParameter);
        }
    }
    if (found.has(EXTENSION_RENEGOTIATION_INFO)) {
        bytes info = found.data_of(EXTENSION_RENEGOTIATION_INFO);
        if (info.size() != 1 || info[0] != 0) {
            return Result<ServerChoice, Error>.Err(Error.BadRenegotiationInfo);
        }
    }
    if (!found.has(EXTENSION_EXTENDED_MASTER_SECRET)) {
        return Result<ServerChoice, Error>.Err(Error.MissingExtendedMasterSecret);
    }
    if (found.data_of(EXTENSION_EXTENDED_MASTER_SECRET).size() != 0) {
        return Result<ServerChoice, Error>.Err(Error.IllegalParameter);
    }
    bytes none = [];
    return Result<ServerChoice, Error>.Ok(ServerChoice(true, tls13schedule.Suite.ChaCha20Poly1305Sha256, suite, none, server_random, false, found.has(EXTENSION_STATUS_REQUEST)));
}

// --- the rest of the server's flight ------------------------------------------------

Result<void, Error> check_encrypted_extensions(bytes message) {
    Reader reader = Reader(message_body(message));
    bytes extension_block = reader.vector16()?;
    if (reader.remaining() != 0) {
        return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.Malformed);
    }
    Reader extensions = Reader(extension_block);
    List<int> seen = [];
    while (extensions.remaining() > 0) {
        int kind = extensions.u16()?;
        bytes data = extensions.vector16()?;
        if (seen.contains(kind)) {
            return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.IllegalParameter);
        }
        seen.push(kind);
        if (kind != EXTENSION_SERVER_NAME && kind != EXTENSION_SUPPORTED_GROUPS) {
            return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.UnsupportedExtension);
        }
    }
    return Result<void, Error>.Ok;
}

// Every certificate of the Certificate message, the server's own first. In
// TLS 1.2 (`legacy`) the message has no request context and no per-entry
// extensions (RFC 5246 §7.4.2). An
// entry after the first that does not parse is dropped: only a parsed
// certificate can take part in a path, so it can neither help nor hurt, and a
// server that sends a spare it cannot be faulted for is still served. The
// first entry has to parse.
Result<List<x509.Certificate>, Error> certificate_list(bytes message, bool legacy) {
    Reader reader = Reader(message_body(message));
    if (!legacy) {
        bytes request_context = reader.vector8()?;
        if (request_context.size() != 0) {
            return Result<List<x509.Certificate>, Error>.Err(Error.Malformed);
        }
    }
    bytes list = reader.vector24()?;
    if (reader.remaining() != 0) {
        return Result<List<x509.Certificate>, Error>.Err(Error.Malformed);
    }
    Reader entries = Reader(list);
    List<x509.Certificate> certificates = [];
    int count = 0;
    while (entries.remaining() > 0) {
        bytes encoded = entries.vector24()?;
        if (!legacy) {
            bytes entry_extensions = entries.vector16()?;
        }
        match (x509.parse(encoded)) {
            case Ok(x509.Certificate certificate): {
                certificates.push(certificate);
            }
            case Err(x509.Error parse_error): {
                if (count == 0) {
                    return Result<List<x509.Certificate>, Error>.Err(Error.BadCertificate);
                }
            }
        }
        count = count + 1;
    }
    if (count == 0) {
        return Result<List<x509.Certificate>, Error>.Err(Error.BadCertificate);
    }
    return Result<List<x509.Certificate>, Error>.Ok(certificates);
}

Result<void, Error> check_pin(x509.Certificate leaf, bytes pinned) {
    bytes key_hash = sha256.sum(leaf.subject_public_key_info);
    if (!consttime.is_equal(key_hash, pinned)) {
        return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.UntrustedKey);
    }
    return Result<void, Error>.Ok;
}

Error error_of_chain(x509chain.Error error) {
    match (error) {
        case EmptyChain: {
            return Error.BadCertificate;
        }
        case UnknownAuthority: {
            return Error.UnknownAuthority;
        }
        case Expired: {
            return Error.CertificateExpired;
        }
        case NotYetValid: {
            return Error.CertificateNotYetValid;
        }
        case HostnameMismatch: {
            return Error.HostnameMismatch;
        }
        case NotCertificateAuthority: {
            return Error.InvalidChain;
        }
        case PathLengthExceeded: {
            return Error.InvalidChain;
        }
        case WrongPurpose: {
            return Error.InvalidChain;
        }
        case BadSignature: {
            return Error.InvalidChain;
        }
        case WeakAlgorithm: {
            return Error.WeakCertificate;
        }
        case UnsupportedAlgorithm: {
            return Error.WeakCertificate;
        }
        case TooDeep: {
            return Error.InvalidChain;
        }
    }
}

// --- trust anchors ----------------------------------------------------------------------

List<str> system_bundle_paths() {
    return ["/etc/pki/tls/certs/ca-bundle.crt", "/etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt", "/etc/ssl/cert.pem"];
}

// The roots in one PEM file; a file that cannot be read, is not PEM, or holds
// no root that parses is `TrustStore`.
Result<List<x509.Certificate>, Error> read_anchors(str path) {
    match (io.read(path)) {
        case Err(io.Error read_error): {
            return Result<List<x509.Certificate>, Error>.Err(Error.TrustStore);
        }
        case Ok(str text): {
            match (x509chain.parse_anchors(text)) {
                case Err(x509.Error pem_error): {
                    return Result<List<x509.Certificate>, Error>.Err(Error.TrustStore);
                }
                case Ok(x509chain.AnchorSet found): {
                    if (found.certificates.size() == 0) {
                        return Result<List<x509.Certificate>, Error>.Err(Error.TrustStore);
                    }
                    return Result<List<x509.Certificate>, Error>.Ok(found.certificates);
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

Result<List<x509.Certificate>, Error> system_anchors() {
    match (os.env("SSL_CERT_FILE")) {
        case Some(str override_path): {
            if (override_path.size() > 0) {
                return read_anchors(override_path);
            }
        }
        case None: {
        }
    }
    for (str path in system_bundle_paths()) {
        match (read_anchors(path)) {
            case Ok(List<x509.Certificate> found): {
                return Result<List<x509.Certificate>, Error>.Ok(found);
            }
            case Err(Error read_error): {
            }
        }
    }
    return Result<List<x509.Certificate>, Error>.Err(Error.TrustStore);
}

// The roots `trust` names, none for a pin. Read before the connection is
// made, so a missing bundle costs no round trip.
Result<List<x509.Certificate>, Error> load_anchors(Trust trust) {
    match (trust) {
        case PinnedKey(bytes pinned): {
            List<x509.Certificate> none = [];
            return Result<List<x509.Certificate>, Error>.Ok(none);
        }
        case SystemRoots: {
            return system_anchors();
        }
        case CaFile(str path): {
            return read_anchors(path);
        }
        case RequireStapled(Trust inner): {
            return load_anchors(inner);
        }
    }
}

// Whether the server's certificates are who `trust` says: the pin matches the
// leaf's key, or a chain leads from the leaf to an anchor and covers `host`.
// The chain's path (leaf first, anchor last) is what comes back, for the
// revocation check; under a pin there is none and the list is empty.
Result<List<x509.Certificate>, Error> check_identity(List<x509.Certificate> presented, Trust trust, List<x509.Certificate> anchors, str host, int now) {
    match (trust) {
        case PinnedKey(bytes pinned): {
            check_pin(presented[0], pinned)?;
            List<x509.Certificate> none = [];
            return Result<List<x509.Certificate>, Error>.Ok(none);
        }
        case SystemRoots: {
            return check_chain(presented, anchors, host, now);
        }
        case CaFile(str path): {
            return check_chain(presented, anchors, host, now);
        }
        case RequireStapled(Trust inner): {
            return check_identity(presented, inner, anchors, host, now);
        }
    }
}

Result<List<x509.Certificate>, Error> check_chain(List<x509.Certificate> presented, List<x509.Certificate> anchors, str host, int now) {
    match (x509chain.verify(presented, anchors, host, now)) {
        case Ok(List<x509.Certificate> path): {
            return Result<List<x509.Certificate>, Error>.Ok(path);
        }
        case Err(x509chain.Error chain_error): {
            return Result<List<x509.Certificate>, Error>.Err(error_of_chain(chain_error));
        }
    }
}

// Whether `trust` demands a stapled response of every server.
bool demands_staple(Trust trust) {
    match (trust) {
        case PinnedKey(bytes pinned): {
            return false;
        }
        case SystemRoots: {
            return false;
        }
        case CaFile(str path): {
            return false;
        }
        case RequireStapled(Trust inner): {
            return true;
        }
    }
}

// The staple policy (see "revocation" in the header). `staple` is the OCSP
// response the server sent for its leaf, if any; `path` is the validated
// chain, empty under a pin. What comes back is the instant after which the
// answer no longer holds (a nextUpdate, epoch seconds), or -1 when no staple
// was used and nothing bounds a session.
Result<int, Error> check_revocation(Trust trust, List<x509.Certificate> path, Option<bytes> staple, int now) {
    if (path.size() == 0) {
        return Result<int, Error>.Ok(-1);
    }
    x509.Certificate leaf = path[0];
    bool required = leaf.must_staple || demands_staple(trust);
    match (staple) {
        case None: {
            if (required) {
                return Result<int, Error>.Err(Error.StapleRequired);
            }
            return Result<int, Error>.Ok(-1);
        }
        case Some(bytes response): {
            if (path.size() < 2) {
                if (required) {
                    return Result<int, Error>.Err(Error.StapleRequired);
                }
                return Result<int, Error>.Ok(-1);
            }
            match (ocsp.check(response, leaf, path[1], now)) {
                case Ok(ocsp.Verdict verdict): {
                    return Result<int, Error>.Ok(verdict.next_update);
                }
                case Err(ocsp.Error failure): {
                    match (failure) {
                        case Revoked: {
                            return Result<int, Error>.Err(Error.CertificateRevoked);
                        }
                        case Unsuccessful: {
                            if (required) {
                                return Result<int, Error>.Err(Error.StapleRequired);
                            }
                            return Result<int, Error>.Ok(-1);
                        }
                        case Malformed: {
                            return Result<int, Error>.Err(Error.BadStapledResponse);
                        }
                        case UnknownCriticalExtension: {
                            return Result<int, Error>.Err(Error.BadStapledResponse);
                        }
                        case UnsupportedAlgorithm: {
                            return Result<int, Error>.Err(Error.BadStapledResponse);
                        }
                        case UnauthorizedResponder: {
                            return Result<int, Error>.Err(Error.BadStapledResponse);
                        }
                        case BadSignature: {
                            return Result<int, Error>.Err(Error.BadStapledResponse);
                        }
                        case WrongCertificate: {
                            return Result<int, Error>.Err(Error.BadStapledResponse);
                        }
                        case NotYetValid: {
                            return Result<int, Error>.Err(Error.BadStapledResponse);
                        }
                        case Stale: {
                            return Result<int, Error>.Err(Error.BadStapledResponse);
                        }
                        case UnknownCertificate: {
                            return Result<int, Error>.Err(Error.BadStapledResponse);
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

// The OCSP response in the status_request extension of the first (the
// server's own) CertificateEntry of a TLS 1.3 Certificate message
// (RFC 8446 §4.4.2.1): `CertificateStatus { status_type = ocsp(1),
// opaque OCSPResponse<1..2^24-1> }`. Extensions of later entries (staples
// for intermediates) are not read, and extensions of the first that are not
// status_request are skipped; a status_request twice, a status_type other
// than ocsp, an empty response or any trailing octet is `Malformed`.
Result<Option<bytes>, Error> stapled_response(bytes message) {
    Reader reader = Reader(message_body(message));
    bytes request_context = reader.vector8()?;
    bytes list = reader.vector24()?;
    Reader entries = Reader(list);
    Option<bytes> found = Option<bytes>.None;
    if (entries.remaining() == 0) {
        return Result<Option<bytes>, Error>.Ok(found);
    }
    bytes encoded = entries.vector24()?;
    Reader extensions = Reader(entries.vector16()?);
    while (extensions.remaining() > 0) {
        int kind = extensions.u16()?;
        bytes data = extensions.vector16()?;
        if (kind == EXTENSION_STATUS_REQUEST) {
            match (found) {
                case Some(bytes earlier): {
                    return Result<Option<bytes>, Error>.Err(Error.Malformed);
                }
                case None: {
                    found = Option<bytes>.Some(certificate_status(data)?);
                }
            }
        }
    }
    return Result<Option<bytes>, Error>.Ok(found);
}

// `CertificateStatus` as TLS 1.2 sends it as a handshake message body and
// TLS 1.3 as extension data: the OCSP response inside.
Result<bytes, Error> certificate_status(bytes data) {
    Reader reader = Reader(data);
    int status_type = reader.u8()?;
    if (status_type != STATUS_TYPE_OCSP) {
        return Result<bytes, Error>.Err(Error.Malformed);
    }
    bytes response = reader.vector24()?;
    if (response.size() == 0 || reader.remaining() != 0) {
        return Result<bytes, Error>.Err(Error.Malformed);
    }
    return Result<bytes, Error>.Ok(response);
}

// What a CertificateVerify signs (RFC 8446 §4.4.3): 64 spaces, a context
// string, a zero, and the transcript hash up to the Certificate.
bytes certificate_verify_content(bytes transcript_hash) {
    bytes content = [0x20; 64];
    content.extend("TLS 1.3, server CertificateVerify".to_bytes());
    content.push(0);
    content.extend(transcript_hash);
    return content;
}

Result<void, Error> verify_signature(ecdsa.Curve curve, str curve_oid, x509.Certificate leaf, bytes digest, bytes signature) {
    if (leaf.public_key.algorithm != x509.OID_EC_PUBLIC_KEY || leaf.public_key.curve != curve_oid) {
        return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.UnsupportedSignature);
    }
    match (ecdsa.parse_public_key(curve, leaf.public_key.key_bytes)) {
        case None: {
            return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.BadCertificate);
        }
        case Some(ecdsa.PublicKey key): {
            if (!ecdsa.verify_der(curve, key, digest, signature)) {
                return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.BadSignature);
            }
            return Result<void, Error>.Ok;
        }
    }
}

Result<void, Error> verify_rsa_pss(x509.Certificate leaf, rsa.Hash hash, bytes digest, bytes signature) {
    if (leaf.public_key.algorithm != x509.OID_RSA_ENCRYPTION) {
        return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.UnsupportedSignature);
    }
    match (rsa.parse_public_key(leaf.public_key.modulus, leaf.public_key.public_exponent)) {
        case None: {
            return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.BadCertificate);
        }
        case Some(rsa.PublicKey key): {
            if (!rsa.verify_pss(key, hash, digest, signature)) {
                return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.BadSignature);
            }
            return Result<void, Error>.Ok;
        }
    }
}

Result<void, Error> verify_rsa_pkcs1(x509.Certificate leaf, rsa.Hash hash, bytes digest, bytes signature) {
    if (leaf.public_key.algorithm != x509.OID_RSA_ENCRYPTION) {
        return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.UnsupportedSignature);
    }
    match (rsa.parse_public_key(leaf.public_key.modulus, leaf.public_key.public_exponent)) {
        case None: {
            return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.BadCertificate);
        }
        case Some(rsa.PublicKey key): {
            if (!rsa.verify_pkcs1_v15(key, hash, digest, signature)) {
                return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.BadSignature);
            }
            return Result<void, Error>.Ok;
        }
    }
}

// Ed25519 (RFC 8032, pure mode) signs the content itself, not a digest of
// it (RFC 8446 §4.2.3), and only an Ed25519 certificate key can have made it.
Result<void, Error> verify_ed25519_signature(x509.Certificate leaf, bytes content, bytes signature) {
    if (leaf.public_key.algorithm != x509.OBJECT_ID_ED25519) {
        return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.UnsupportedSignature);
    }
    if (!ed25519.verify(leaf.public_key.key_bytes, content, signature)) {
        return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.BadSignature);
    }
    return Result<void, Error>.Ok;
}

Result<void, Error> check_certificate_verify(bytes message, x509.Certificate leaf, bytes transcript_hash) {
    Reader reader = Reader(message_body(message));
    int scheme = reader.u16()?;
    bytes signature = reader.vector16()?;
    if (reader.remaining() != 0) {
        return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.Malformed);
    }
    bytes content = certificate_verify_content(transcript_hash);
    if (scheme == SCHEME_ECDSA_P256_SHA256) {
        return verify_signature(ecdsa.p256(), x509.OID_CURVE_P256, leaf, sha256.sum(content), signature);
    }
    if (scheme == SCHEME_ECDSA_P384_SHA384) {
        return verify_signature(ecdsa.p384(), x509.OID_CURVE_P384, leaf, sha512.sum384(content), signature);
    }
    if (scheme == SCHEME_ED25519) {
        return verify_ed25519_signature(leaf, content, signature);
    }
    if (scheme == SCHEME_RSA_PSS_RSAE_SHA256) {
        return verify_rsa_pss(leaf, rsa.Hash.Sha256, sha256.sum(content), signature);
    }
    if (scheme == SCHEME_RSA_PSS_RSAE_SHA384) {
        return verify_rsa_pss(leaf, rsa.Hash.Sha384, sha512.sum384(content), signature);
    }
    if (scheme == SCHEME_RSA_PSS_RSAE_SHA512) {
        return verify_rsa_pss(leaf, rsa.Hash.Sha512, sha512.sum(content), signature);
    }
    return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.UnsupportedSignature);
}

Result<void, Error> check_finished(tls13schedule.Suite suite, bytes message, bytes expected) {
    bytes verify_data = message_body(message);
    if (verify_data.size() != suite.hash_size()) {
        return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.Malformed);
    }
    if (!consttime.is_equal(verify_data, expected)) {
        return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.BadFinished);
    }
    return Result<void, Error>.Ok;
}

Result<void, Error> require_type(bytes message, int wanted) {
    if (message[0] != wanted) {
        return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.UnexpectedMessage);
    }
    return Result<void, Error>.Ok;
}

// --- the handshake ------------------------------------------------------------------------

Result<void, Error> lift(Result<void, tls13record.Error> outcome) {
    match (outcome) {
        case Ok: {
            return Result<void, Error>.Ok;
        }
        case Err(tls13record.Error record_error): {
            return Result<void, Error>.Err(error_of_record(record_error));
        }
    }
}

Result<bytes, Error> next_handshake(tls13record.Connection record) {
    match (record.receive_handshake()) {
        case Ok(bytes message): {
            return Result<bytes, Error>.Ok(message);
        }
        case Err(tls13record.Error record_error): {
            return Result<bytes, Error>.Err(error_of_record(record_error));
        }
    }
}

// What a finished handshake leaves for the connection: whether it was a
// resumption, and, for a TLS 1.3 handshake, what NewSessionTickets turn into
// sessions.
type Outcome {
    bool resumed = false;
    bool ticketable = false;
    tls13schedule.Suite suite = tls13schedule.Suite.ChaCha20Poly1305Sha256;
    bytes resumption_master = [];
    int valid_until = 0;
    // When the stapled OCSP response that vouched for the server's
    // certificate stops holding, or -1 if none did.
    int staple_expires = -1;
}

// What `trust` is, as text, for filing sessions: two connections share a
// session only when this and the host are the same.
str trust_key(Trust trust) {
    match (trust) {
        case PinnedKey(bytes pinned): {
            str text = "pin";
            for (int index in 0 .. pinned.size()) {
                text = text + ":" + pinned[index].to_str();
            }
            return text;
        }
        case SystemRoots: {
            return "system";
        }
        case CaFile(str path): {
            return "ca:" + path;
        }
        case RequireStapled(Trust inner): {
            return "stapled:" + trust_key(inner);
        }
    }
}

// What a resumed session is bound to besides the host: the trust, and the
// client certificate (if any) that earned it. A session earned with one
// identity is never offered under another, or under none.
str session_key(Trust trust, Option<clientcert.Identity> identity) {
    match (identity) {
        case Some(clientcert.Identity presenting): {
            return trust_key(trust) + "|client:" + presenting.fingerprint();
        }
        case None: {
            return trust_key(trust);
        }
    }
}

bool is_pinned(Trust trust) {
    match (trust) {
        case PinnedKey(bytes pinned): {
            return true;
        }
        case SystemRoots: {
            return false;
        }
        case CaFile(str path): {
            return false;
        }
        case RequireStapled(Trust inner): {
            return is_pinned(inner);
        }
    }
}

Result<void, Error> run_handshake(tls13record.Connection record, str host, Trust trust, List<x509.Certificate> anchors, int now, tlsresume.SessionCache cache, Outcome outcome, Option<clientcert.Identity> identity) {
    random.System source = random.system();
    bytes secret = source.octets(X25519_SIZE);
    bytes session_id = source.octets(SESSION_ID_SIZE);
    bytes client_random = source.octets(RANDOM_SIZE);
    str key = session_key(trust, identity);
    Option<tlsresume.Session> offered = cache.take(host, key, now);
    bool offered_psk = false;
    match (offered) {
        case Some(tlsresume.Session candidate): {
            offered_psk = true;
        }
        case None: {
        }
    }
    bytes hello = client_hello(host, client_random, session_id, x25519.base_point(secret), cache.enabled(), offered, now);
    tls13schedule.Transcript transcript = tls13schedule.transcript();
    transcript.add(hello);
    lift(record.send_handshake(hello))?;
    bytes server_hello = next_handshake(record)?;
    require_type(server_hello, HANDSHAKE_SERVER_HELLO)?;
    ServerChoice choice = check_server_hello(server_hello, session_id, !is_ip_literal(host), offered_psk)?;
    transcript.add(server_hello);
    if (choice.legacy) {
        return run_handshake_12(record, choice, transcript, client_random, secret, source, host, trust, anchors, now, outcome);
    }
    tls13schedule.Suite suite = choice.suite;
    bytes server_share = choice.public_key;
    record.use_suite(suite);
    bytes shared = x25519.x25519(secret, server_share);
    if (all_zero(shared)) {
        return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.IllegalParameter);
    }
    lift(record.send_change_cipher_spec())?;
    bytes psk = [];
    int valid_until = now + tlsresume.MAX_LIFETIME;
    if (choice.psk_selected) {
        match (offered) {
            case Some(tlsresume.Session accepted): {
                if (accepted.suite.code() != suite.code()) {
                    return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.IllegalParameter);
                }
                psk = accepted.psk;
                valid_until = accepted.valid_until;
            }
            case None: {
                return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.IllegalParameter);
            }
        }
    }
    bytes early = tls13schedule.early_secret(suite, psk);
    bytes handshake = tls13schedule.handshake_secret(suite, early, shared);
    bytes hello_hash = transcript.digest(suite);
    bytes client_handshake = tls13schedule.client_handshake_traffic_secret(suite, handshake, hello_hash);
    bytes server_handshake = tls13schedule.server_handshake_traffic_secret(suite, handshake, hello_hash);
    lift(record.install_read_keys(server_handshake))?;
    record.install_write_keys(client_handshake);
    bytes encrypted_extensions = next_handshake(record)?;
    require_type(encrypted_extensions, HANDSHAKE_ENCRYPTED_EXTENSIONS)?;
    check_encrypted_extensions(encrypted_extensions)?;
    transcript.add(encrypted_extensions);
    // A resumed handshake is authenticated by the PSK alone: no Certificate,
    // no CertificateRequest, no CertificateVerify (RFC 8446 §2.2). A full one
    // validates the server's chain exactly as before.
    bool requested = false;
    if (!choice.psk_selected) {
        bytes certificate_message = next_handshake(record)?;
        if (certificate_message[0] == HANDSHAKE_CERTIFICATE_REQUEST) {
            match (identity) {
                case None: {
                    return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.ClientCertificateRequested);
                }
                case Some(clientcert.Identity presenting): {
                    check_certificate_request(certificate_message, presenting)?;
                }
            }
            transcript.add(certificate_message);
            requested = true;
            certificate_message = next_handshake(record)?;
        }
        require_type(certificate_message, HANDSHAKE_CERTIFICATE)?;
        List<x509.Certificate> presented = certificate_list(certificate_message, false)?;
        x509.Certificate leaf = presented[0];
        List<x509.Certificate> path = check_identity(presented, trust, anchors, host, now)?;
        int answer_expires = check_revocation(trust, path, stapled_response(certificate_message)?, now)?;
        outcome.staple_expires = answer_expires;
        if (answer_expires >= 0 && answer_expires < valid_until) {
            valid_until = answer_expires;
        }
        if (!is_pinned(trust)) {
            for (x509.Certificate each in presented) {
                if (each.not_after < valid_until) {
                    valid_until = each.not_after;
                }
            }
        }
        transcript.add(certificate_message);
        bytes certificate_hash = transcript.digest(suite);
        bytes verify_message = next_handshake(record)?;
        require_type(verify_message, HANDSHAKE_CERTIFICATE_VERIFY)?;
        check_certificate_verify(verify_message, leaf, certificate_hash)?;
        transcript.add(verify_message);
    }
    bytes server_finished = next_handshake(record)?;
    require_type(server_finished, HANDSHAKE_FINISHED)?;
    bytes server_key = tls13schedule.finished_key(suite, server_handshake);
    check_finished(suite, server_finished, tls13schedule.finished_verify_data(suite, server_key, transcript.digest(suite)))?;
    transcript.add(server_finished);
    bytes finished_hash = transcript.digest(suite);
    bytes master = tls13schedule.master_secret(suite, handshake);
    bytes client_application = tls13schedule.client_application_traffic_secret(suite, master, finished_hash);
    bytes server_application = tls13schedule.server_application_traffic_secret(suite, master, finished_hash);
    if (requested) {
        match (identity) {
            case Some(clientcert.Identity presenting): {
                send_client_certificate(record, transcript, suite, presenting)?;
            }
            case None: {
                trap("tls: a certificate was requested of a client with none");
            }
        }
    }
    bytes client_key = tls13schedule.finished_key(suite, client_handshake);
    bytes client_finished = [HANDSHAKE_FINISHED, 0, 0, suite.hash_size()];
    client_finished.extend(tls13schedule.finished_verify_data(suite, client_key, transcript.digest(suite)));
    lift(record.send_handshake(client_finished))?;
    record.install_write_keys(client_application);
    lift(record.install_read_keys(server_application))?;
    transcript.add(client_finished);
    outcome.resumed = choice.psk_selected;
    outcome.ticketable = true;
    outcome.suite = suite;
    outcome.resumption_master = tls13schedule.resumption_master_secret(suite, master, transcript.digest(suite));
    outcome.valid_until = valid_until;
    return Result<void, Error>.Ok;
}

// RFC 8446 §4.3.2: a CertificateRequest in a handshake has an empty
// certificate_request_context, and extensions that must include
// signature_algorithms (once -- no extension may repeat). Anything else is a
// malformed or illegal message. The identity must be able to sign with one of
// the schemes listed, or the handshake ends: this client does not send an
// empty Certificate to a server that asked for one, so a server that would
// have accepted none is not silently served as if it had not asked.
// certificate_authorities, if present, is parsed and then not used: one
// identity is all the caller gave.
Result<void, Error> check_certificate_request(bytes message, clientcert.Identity identity) {
    Reader reader = Reader(message_body(message));
    bytes context = reader.vector8()?;
    if (context.size() != 0) {
        return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.IllegalParameter);
    }
    bytes extensions = reader.vector16()?;
    if (reader.remaining() != 0) {
        return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.Malformed);
    }
    Reader block = Reader(extensions);
    List<int> seen = [];
    bool found = false;
    bool usable = false;
    while (block.remaining() > 0) {
        int kind = block.u16()?;
        bytes data = block.vector16()?;
        for (int earlier in seen) {
            if (earlier == kind) {
                return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.IllegalParameter);
            }
        }
        seen.push(kind);
        if (kind == EXTENSION_SIGNATURE_ALGORITHMS) {
            found = true;
            Reader list = Reader(data);
            bytes pairs = list.vector16()?;
            if (list.remaining() != 0 || pairs.size() == 0 || pairs.size() % 2 != 0) {
                return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.Malformed);
            }
            for (int index in 0 .. pairs.size() / 2) {
                if (pairs[2 * index] * 256 + pairs[2 * index + 1] == identity.signature_scheme()) {
                    usable = true;
                }
            }
        }
    }
    if (!found) {
        return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.IllegalParameter);
    }
    if (!usable) {
        return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.ClientCertificateUnusable);
    }
    return Result<void, Error>.Ok;
}

// What a client CertificateVerify signs: as the server's (above), with the
// client's context string, over the transcript up to the client Certificate.
bytes client_certificate_verify_content(bytes transcript_hash) {
    bytes content = [0x20; 64];
    content.extend("TLS 1.3, client CertificateVerify".to_bytes());
    content.push(0);
    content.extend(transcript_hash);
    return content;
}

// Sends the client's Certificate (empty request context, the chain with no
// per-certificate extensions) and CertificateVerify, adding both to the
// transcript; the client Finished follows over the transcript so far.
Result<void, Error> send_client_certificate(tls13record.Connection record, tls13schedule.Transcript transcript, tls13schedule.Suite suite, clientcert.Identity identity) {
    bytes entries = [];
    for (bytes der_certificate in identity.chain) {
        put_u24(entries, der_certificate.size());
        entries.extend(der_certificate);
        put_u16(entries, 0);
    }
    bytes certificate_body = [0];
    put_u24(certificate_body, entries.size());
    certificate_body.extend(entries);
    bytes certificate = [HANDSHAKE_CERTIFICATE];
    put_u24(certificate, certificate_body.size());
    certificate.extend(certificate_body);
    transcript.add(certificate);
    lift(record.send_handshake(certificate))?;
    match (identity.sign(client_certificate_verify_content(transcript.digest(suite)))) {
        case Err(signingkey.Error sign_error): {
            return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.UnsupportedSignature);
        }
        case Ok(bytes signature): {
            bytes verify_body = [];
            put_u16(verify_body, identity.signature_scheme());
            put_u16(verify_body, signature.size());
            verify_body.extend(signature);
            bytes verify = [HANDSHAKE_CERTIFICATE_VERIFY];
            put_u24(verify, verify_body.size());
            verify.extend(verify_body);
            transcript.add(verify);
            lift(record.send_handshake(verify))?;
        }
    }
    return Result<void, Error>.Ok;
}

// --- TLS 1.2 ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// The server's ECDHE parameters, signature checked: its group and public point.
type KeyExchange {
    int group;
    bytes point;
}

// ECDSA over a TLS 1.2 key exchange. TLS 1.2 leaves the curve to the
// certificate (RFC 8446 §4.2.3 note on ecdsa_secp256r1_sha256 in 1.2), and
// real servers pair a P-384 key with SHA-256, so the scheme gives the hash and
// the certificate's own key the curve -- P-256 or P-384, nothing else.
Result<void, Error> verify_ecdsa_12(x509.Certificate leaf, int scheme, bytes content, bytes signature) {
    bytes digest = sha256.sum(content);
    if (scheme == SCHEME_ECDSA_P384_SHA384) {
        digest = sha512.sum384(content);
    }
    if (leaf.public_key.algorithm == x509.OID_EC_PUBLIC_KEY && leaf.public_key.curve == x509.OID_CURVE_P256) {
        return verify_signature(ecdsa.p256(), x509.OID_CURVE_P256, leaf, digest, signature);
    }
    if (leaf.public_key.algorithm == x509.OID_EC_PUBLIC_KEY && leaf.public_key.curve == x509.OID_CURVE_P384) {
        return verify_signature(ecdsa.p384(), x509.OID_CURVE_P384, leaf, digest, signature);
    }
    return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.UnsupportedSignature);
}

// The signature over a ServerKeyExchange: a scheme that was offered, that
// fits the suite's authentication (an ECDSA suite is signed with ECDSA, an RSA
// suite with RSA-PSS or PKCS#1 v1.5), and that verifies under the leaf's key.
Result<void, Error> verify_key_exchange_signature(tls12.Suite suite, x509.Certificate leaf, int scheme, bytes content, bytes signature) {
    if (suite.is_ecdsa()) {
        if (scheme == SCHEME_ED25519) {
            return verify_ed25519_signature(leaf, content, signature);
        }
        if (scheme == SCHEME_ECDSA_P256_SHA256 || scheme == SCHEME_ECDSA_P384_SHA384) {
            return verify_ecdsa_12(leaf, scheme, content, signature);
        }
        return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.UnsupportedSignature);
    }
    if (scheme == SCHEME_RSA_PSS_RSAE_SHA256) {
        return verify_rsa_pss(leaf, rsa.Hash.Sha256, sha256.sum(content), signature);
    }
    if (scheme == SCHEME_RSA_PSS_RSAE_SHA384) {
        return verify_rsa_pss(leaf, rsa.Hash.Sha384, sha512.sum384(content), signature);
    }
    if (scheme == SCHEME_RSA_PSS_RSAE_SHA512) {
        return verify_rsa_pss(leaf, rsa.Hash.Sha512, sha512.sum(content), signature);
    }
    if (scheme == SCHEME_RSA_PKCS1_SHA256) {
        return verify_rsa_pkcs1(leaf, rsa.Hash.Sha256, sha256.sum(content), signature);
    }
    if (scheme == SCHEME_RSA_PKCS1_SHA384) {
        return verify_rsa_pkcs1(leaf, rsa.Hash.Sha384, sha512.sum384(content), signature);
    }
    if (scheme == SCHEME_RSA_PKCS1_SHA512) {
        return verify_rsa_pkcs1(leaf, rsa.Hash.Sha512, sha512.sum(content), signature);
    }
    return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.UnsupportedSignature);
}

// RFC 8422 §5.4: `curve_type` (named_curve, 3), the group, the point, then the
// signature over the randoms and those parameters.
Result<KeyExchange, Error> check_server_key_exchange(bytes message, tls12.Suite suite, x509.Certificate leaf, bytes client_random, bytes server_random) {
    bytes body = message_body(message);
    Reader reader = Reader(body);
    int curve_type = reader.u8()?;
    int group = reader.u16()?;
    bytes point = reader.vector8()?;
    int params_size = reader.at;
    int scheme = reader.u16()?;
    bytes signature = reader.vector16()?;
    if (reader.remaining() != 0) {
        return Result<KeyExchange, Error>.Err(Error.Malformed);
    }
    if (curve_type != CURVE_TYPE_NAMED) {
        return Result<KeyExchange, Error>.Err(Error.IllegalParameter);
    }
    if (!(group == GROUP_X25519 && point.size() == X25519_SIZE) && !(group == GROUP_SECP256R1 && point.size() == P256_POINT_SIZE)) {
        return Result<KeyExchange, Error>.Err(Error.IllegalParameter);
    }
    bytes content = tls12.signed_params(client_random, server_random, body.substr(0, params_size));
    verify_key_exchange_signature(suite, leaf, scheme, content, signature)?;
    return Result<KeyExchange, Error>.Ok(KeyExchange(group, point));
}

// What the client sends in ClientKeyExchange and the premaster secret that
// comes of it: a fresh x25519 or P-256 key against the server's.
type ClientShare {
    bytes public_point;
    bytes premaster;
}

Result<ClientShare, Error> client_key_share(KeyExchange exchange, bytes x25519_secret, random.System source) {
    if (exchange.group == GROUP_X25519) {
        bytes shared = x25519.x25519(x25519_secret, exchange.point);
        if (all_zero(shared)) {
            return Result<ClientShare, Error>.Err(Error.IllegalParameter);
        }
        return Result<ClientShare, Error>.Ok(ClientShare(x25519.base_point(x25519_secret), shared));
    }
    ecdsa.Curve curve = ecdsa.p256();
    match (ecdsa.parse_public_key(curve, exchange.point)) {
        case None: {
            return Result<ClientShare, Error>.Err(Error.IllegalParameter);
        }
        case Some(ecdsa.PublicKey peer): {
            bytes scalar = source.octets(curve.size);
            while (!ecdsa.is_secret_scalar(curve, scalar)) {
                scalar = source.octets(curve.size);
            }
            return Result<ClientShare, Error>.Ok(ClientShare(ecdsa.ephemeral_public(curve, scalar), ecdsa.shared_secret(curve, peer, scalar)));
        }
    }
}

Result<void, Error> check_finished_12(bytes message, bytes expected) {
    bytes verify_data = message_body(message);
    if (verify_data.size() != tls12.VERIFY_DATA_SIZE) {
        return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.Malformed);
    }
    if (!consttime.is_equal(verify_data, expected)) {
        return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.BadFinished);
    }
    return Result<void, Error>.Ok;
}

// The rest of a TLS 1.2 handshake, once the ServerHello (already in
// `transcript`) has named a suite: the server's flight up to ServerHelloDone,
// our ClientKeyExchange, ChangeCipherSpec and Finished, and the server's.
Result<void, Error> run_handshake_12(tls13record.Connection record, ServerChoice choice, tls13schedule.Transcript transcript, bytes client_random, bytes x25519_secret, random.System source, str host, Trust trust, List<x509.Certificate> anchors, int now, Outcome outcome) {
    tls12.Suite suite = choice.legacy_suite;
    tls13schedule.Suite aead = suite.aead();
    record.use_legacy_suite(aead);
    bytes certificate_message = next_handshake(record)?;
    require_type(certificate_message, HANDSHAKE_CERTIFICATE)?;
    List<x509.Certificate> presented = certificate_list(certificate_message, true)?;
    x509.Certificate leaf = presented[0];
    List<x509.Certificate> path = check_identity(presented, trust, anchors, host, now)?;
    transcript.add(certificate_message);
    bytes exchange_message = next_handshake(record)?;
    Option<bytes> staple = Option<bytes>.None;
    if (exchange_message[0] == HANDSHAKE_CERTIFICATE_STATUS) {
        if (!choice.status_promised) {
            return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.UnexpectedMessage);
        }
        staple = Option<bytes>.Some(certificate_status(message_body(exchange_message))?);
        transcript.add(exchange_message);
        exchange_message = next_handshake(record)?;
    }
    outcome.staple_expires = check_revocation(trust, path, staple, now)?;
    require_type(exchange_message, HANDSHAKE_SERVER_KEY_EXCHANGE)?;
    KeyExchange exchange = check_server_key_exchange(exchange_message, suite, leaf, client_random, choice.server_random)?;
    transcript.add(exchange_message);
    bytes done_message = next_handshake(record)?;
    if (done_message[0] == HANDSHAKE_CERTIFICATE_REQUEST) {
        return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.ClientCertificateRequested);
    }
    require_type(done_message, HANDSHAKE_SERVER_HELLO_DONE)?;
    if (done_message.size() != 4) {
        return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.Malformed);
    }
    transcript.add(done_message);
    ClientShare share = client_key_share(exchange, x25519_secret, source)?;
    bytes key_exchange_body = [share.public_point.size()];
    key_exchange_body.extend(share.public_point);
    bytes key_exchange = [HANDSHAKE_CLIENT_KEY_EXCHANGE];
    put_u24(key_exchange, key_exchange_body.size());
    key_exchange.extend(key_exchange_body);
    lift(record.send_handshake(key_exchange))?;
    transcript.add(key_exchange);
    bytes master = suite.master_secret(share.premaster, transcript.digest(aead));
    tls12.KeyBlock keys = suite.key_block(master, choice.server_random, client_random);
    lift(record.send_change_cipher_spec())?;
    record.install_legacy_write_keys(keys.client_key, keys.client_iv);
    bytes client_finished = [HANDSHAKE_FINISHED, 0, 0, tls12.VERIFY_DATA_SIZE];
    client_finished.extend(suite.verify_data(master, true, transcript.digest(aead)));
    lift(record.send_handshake(client_finished))?;
    transcript.add(client_finished);
    lift(record.receive_change_cipher_spec())?;
    lift(record.install_legacy_read_keys(keys.server_key, keys.server_iv))?;
    bytes server_finished = next_handshake(record)?;
    require_type(server_finished, HANDSHAKE_FINISHED)?;
    check_finished_12(server_finished, suite.verify_data(master, false, transcript.digest(aead)))?;
    return Result<void, Error>.Ok;
}

// A failed handshake: the alert that says why (the record layer has sent its
// own for the faults it finds), then the stream closed.
Error abort_handshake(tls13record.Connection record, Error error) {
    int description = alert_for(error);
    if (description >= 0) {
        Result<void, tls13record.Error> alerted = record.send_alert(description);
    }
    Result<bool, tls13record.Error> closed = record.close();
    return error;
}

/// Connects to `host` on `port`, runs the handshake and checks the server
/// against `trust`. What comes back is a stream of the server's decrypted
/// octets; every `Err` is a connection that does not exist.
pub Result<Conn, Error> connect(str host, int port, Trust trust) {
    return connect_cached(host, port, trust, tlsresume.cache(0));
}

/// `connect`, with session resumption: if `cache` holds a session for exactly
/// this `host` under exactly this `trust` it is offered (once -- it leaves the
/// cache), and the NewSessionTickets the server sends are filed in `cache` as
/// the connection is read. See `tlsresume.m31` and the header of this file.
pub Result<Conn, Error> connect_cached(str host, int port, Trust trust, tlsresume.SessionCache cache) {
    List<x509.Certificate> anchors = load_anchors(trust)?;
    match (net.connect(host, port)) {
        case Err(net.Error connect_error): {
            return Result<Conn, Error>.Err(Error.Connect);
        }
        case Ok(net.Conn socket): {
            return handshake_over(socket, host, trust, anchors, date.now(), false, cache, Option<clientcert.Identity>.None);
        }
    }
}

/// `connect_cached`, with the clock given in epoch seconds: the time the
/// certificates are checked at, the age of the ticket offered and the time
/// new tickets are stamped with. For tests.
pub Result<Conn, Error> connect_cached_at(str host, int port, Trust trust, tlsresume.SessionCache cache, int now) {
    List<x509.Certificate> anchors = load_anchors(trust)?;
    match (net.connect(host, port)) {
        case Err(net.Error connect_error): {
            return Result<Conn, Error>.Err(Error.Connect);
        }
        case Ok(net.Conn socket): {
            return handshake_over(socket, host, trust, anchors, now, true, cache, Option<clientcert.Identity>.None);
        }
    }
}

/// `connect_cached`, over a socket the caller already opened (see
/// `connect_over`).
pub Result<Conn, Error> connect_over_cached(net.Conn socket, str host, Trust trust, tlsresume.SessionCache cache) {
    List<x509.Certificate> anchors = load_anchors(trust)?;
    return handshake_over(socket, host, trust, anchors, date.now(), false, cache, Option<clientcert.Identity>.None);
}

/// `connect_over_cached`, with the clock given in epoch seconds.
pub Result<Conn, Error> connect_over_cached_at(net.Conn socket, str host, Trust trust, tlsresume.SessionCache cache, int now) {
    List<x509.Certificate> anchors = load_anchors(trust)?;
    return handshake_over(socket, host, trust, anchors, now, true, cache, Option<clientcert.Identity>.None);
}

/// `connect_cached`, presenting `identity` if the server asks for a client
/// certificate (TLS 1.3 only; see the header). A server that does not ask never
/// sees it. A session earned under one identity is offered only under that
/// identity.
pub Result<Conn, Error> connect_identified(str host, int port, Trust trust, clientcert.Identity identity, tlsresume.SessionCache cache) {
    List<x509.Certificate> anchors = load_anchors(trust)?;
    match (net.connect(host, port)) {
        case Err(net.Error connect_error): {
            return Result<Conn, Error>.Err(Error.Connect);
        }
        case Ok(net.Conn socket): {
            return handshake_over(socket, host, trust, anchors, date.now(), false, cache, Option<clientcert.Identity>.Some(identity));
        }
    }
}

/// `connect_identified` over a socket the caller already opened, the clock
/// given in epoch seconds.
pub Result<Conn, Error> connect_over_identified_at(net.Conn socket, str host, Trust trust, clientcert.Identity identity, tlsresume.SessionCache cache, int now) {
    List<x509.Certificate> anchors = load_anchors(trust)?;
    return handshake_over(socket, host, trust, anchors, now, true, cache, Option<clientcert.Identity>.Some(identity));
}

/// `connect_identified` over a socket the caller already opened.
pub Result<Conn, Error> connect_over_identified(net.Conn socket, str host, Trust trust, clientcert.Identity identity, tlsresume.SessionCache cache) {
    List<x509.Certificate> anchors = load_anchors(trust)?;
    return handshake_over(socket, host, trust, anchors, date.now(), false, cache, Option<clientcert.Identity>.Some(identity));
}

/// `connect`, with the time certificates are checked against given as epoch
/// seconds instead of read from the clock. For tests, and for a program that
/// has a better clock than the machine's.
pub Result<Conn, Error> connect_at(str host, int port, Trust trust, int now) {
    List<x509.Certificate> anchors = load_anchors(trust)?;
    match (net.connect(host, port)) {
        case Err(net.Error connect_error): {
            return Result<Conn, Error>.Err(Error.Connect);
        }
        case Ok(net.Conn socket): {
            return handshake_over(socket, host, trust, anchors, now, true, tlsresume.cache(0), Option<clientcert.Identity>.None);
        }
    }
}

/// `connect`, over a socket the caller already opened: for a caller that
/// wants `net`'s own error names for the connection, or to set timeouts and
/// options on the socket before the handshake. `host` is what the server's
/// certificate must cover and what goes in SNI; it need not be where
/// `socket` is connected, and a caller that connects somewhere else on
/// purpose is the one who owns that decision. The socket is closed when the
/// handshake fails.
pub Result<Conn, Error> connect_over(net.Conn socket, str host, Trust trust) {
    return connect_over_cached(socket, host, trust, tlsresume.cache(0));
}

/// `connect_over`, with the clock given in epoch seconds.
pub Result<Conn, Error> connect_over_at(net.Conn socket, str host, Trust trust, int now) {
    List<x509.Certificate> anchors = load_anchors(trust)?;
    return handshake_over(socket, host, trust, anchors, now, true, tlsresume.cache(0), Option<clientcert.Identity>.None);
}

/// `connect_over` with a bound on the whole handshake: `deadline_ms` is the
/// total time the handshake (reads and writes together) may take, and
/// `timeout_ms` the longest any one read may wait; 0 means no limit for
/// either. A server that drips one byte at a time inside the per-read limit
/// is cut off at the deadline. Running out of either is `Error.Io`. While the
/// handshake runs this sets the socket's read and write timeouts itself, and
/// it clears both when the handshake ends, so set any timeouts for the
/// connection's life afterwards. The clock is the wall clock; see
/// `deadlinestream`.
pub Result<Conn, Error> connect_over_deadline(net.Conn socket, str host, Trust trust, int deadline_ms, int timeout_ms = 0) {
    List<x509.Certificate> anchors = load_anchors(trust)?;
    deadlinestream.Bounded bounded = deadlinestream.bound(socket, deadline_ms, timeout_ms);
    Result<Conn, Error> opened = handshake_over(bounded, host, trust, anchors, date.now(), false, tlsresume.cache(0), Option<clientcert.Identity>.None);
    match (opened) {
        case Ok(Conn connection): {
            bounded.release();
        }
        case Err(Error handshake_error): {
        }
    }
    return opened;
}

Result<Conn, Error> handshake_over(io.Stream socket, str host, Trust trust, List<x509.Certificate> anchors, int now, bool fixed_clock, tlsresume.SessionCache cache, Option<clientcert.Identity> identity) {
    tls13record.Connection record = tls13record.connection(socket);
    Outcome outcome = Outcome();
    match (run_handshake(record, host, trust, anchors, now, cache, outcome, identity)) {
        case Ok: {
            Conn conn = Conn(record, cache);
            conn.host = host;
            conn.trust_key = session_key(trust, identity);
            conn.outcome = outcome;
            conn.clock = now;
            conn.fixed_clock = fixed_clock;
            return Result<Conn, Error>.Ok(conn);
        }
        case Err(Error handshake_error): {
            return Result<Conn, Error>.Err(abort_handshake(record, handshake_error));
        }
    }
}

// --- the connection ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// A TLS 1.3 connection to a server whose key was checked. Made by
/// `connect`; every field is private.
pub type Conn {
    tls13record.Connection record;
    tlsresume.SessionCache cache;
    // pending[offset ..] has been decrypted and not handed out.
    bytes pending = [];
    int offset = 0;
    bool at_end = false;
    bool closed = false;
    bool failed = false;
    Error failure = Error.Closed;
    // For filing NewSessionTickets as sessions; see `file_tickets`.
    str host = "";
    str trust_key = "";
    Outcome outcome = Outcome();
    int clock = 0;
    bool fixed_clock = false;
}

/// Whether this connection was resumed from a ticket: the server accepted the
/// PSK, and no certificate was exchanged on it.
pub bool Conn.resumed() {
    return outcome.resumed;
}

/// Whether a stapled OCSP response vouched for the server's certificate on
/// this connection: present, signed by its issuer or an authorised responder,
/// fresh, and saying "good". `false` on a resumed connection (no certificate
/// was exchanged) and wherever the staple was absent and not required.
pub bool Conn.stapled() {
    return outcome.staple_expires >= 0;
}

int Conn.now() {
    if (fixed_clock) {
        return clock;
    }
    return date.now();
}

// RFC 8446 §4.6.1: ticket_lifetime(4) ticket_age_add(4) ticket_nonce<0..255>
// ticket<1..2^16-1> extensions<0..2^16-2>. The only extension defined for it
// is early_data, which is parsed over and otherwise ignored: no 0-RTT here. A
// lifetime of 0 means the server does not want the ticket used. A message
// that does not parse is a `decode_error`.
Result<void, Error> Conn.file_ticket(bytes message) {
    Reader reader = Reader(message_body(message));
    int lifetime_high = reader.u16()?;
    int lifetime_low = reader.u16()?;
    int age_high = reader.u16()?;
    int age_low = reader.u16()?;
    int lifetime = lifetime_high * 65536 + lifetime_low;
    int age_add = age_high * 65536 + age_low;
    bytes nonce = reader.vector8()?;
    bytes ticket = reader.vector16()?;
    bytes extension_block = reader.vector16()?;
    if (reader.remaining() != 0 || ticket.size() == 0) {
        return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.Malformed);
    }
    Reader extensions = Reader(extension_block);
    while (extensions.remaining() > 0) {
        int kind = extensions.u16()?;
        bytes data = extensions.vector16()?;
    }
    if (lifetime == 0 || !cache.enabled()) {
        return Result<void, Error>.Ok;
    }
    bytes psk = tls13schedule.resumption_psk(outcome.suite, outcome.resumption_master, nonce);
    int received = now();
    cache.put(tlsresume.session(host, trust_key, outcome.suite, psk, ticket, age_add, received, lifetime, outcome.valid_until), received);
    return Result<void, Error>.Ok;
}

Result<void, Error> Conn.file_tickets() {
    List<bytes> arrived = record.take_tickets();
    if (!outcome.ticketable) {
        return Result<void, Error>.Ok;
    }
    for (bytes message in arrived) {
        file_ticket(message)?;
    }
    return Result<void, Error>.Ok;
}

// The next chunk of application data, or an empty one once the server has
// said it is finished.
Result<bytes, io.Error> Conn.next_chunk() {
    if (failed) {
        return Result<bytes, io.Error>.Err(io.Error.Other(errno_of(failure)));
    }
    if (closed) {
        return Result<bytes, io.Error>.Err(io.Error.Other(ERRNO_BAD_DESCRIPTOR));
    }
    if (at_end) {
        bytes none = [];
        return Result<bytes, io.Error>.Ok(none);
    }
    match (record.receive_application()) {
        case Ok(bytes data): {
            match (file_tickets()) {
                case Ok: {
                }
                case Err(Error ticket_error): {
                    Result<void, tls13record.Error> alerted = record.send_alert(alert_for(ticket_error));
                    return Result<bytes, io.Error>.Err(fail(ticket_error));
                }
            }
            if (data.size() == 0) {
                at_end = true;
            }
            return Result<bytes, io.Error>.Ok(data);
        }
        case Err(tls13record.Error record_error): {
            return Result<bytes, io.Error>.Err(fail(error_of_record(record_error)));
        }
    }
}

io.Error Conn.fail(Error error) {
    failed = true;
    failure = error;
    return io.Error.Other(errno_of(error));
}

/// Between 1 and `n` octets, or an empty `bytes` once the server has sent
/// `close_notify`. `n` below 1 traps.
pub Result<bytes, io.Error> Conn.read(int count) {
    if (count < 1) {
        trap("read(" + count.to_str() + "): read at least one byte; read_all() reads everything");
    }
    if (offset == pending.size()) {
        bytes chunk = next_chunk()?;
        if (chunk.size() == 0) {
            return Result<bytes, io.Error>.Ok(chunk);
        }
        pending = chunk;
        offset = 0;
    }
    int take = pending.size() - offset;
    if (take > count) {
        take = count;
    }
    bytes out = pending.substr(offset, offset + take);
    offset = offset + take;
    return Result<bytes, io.Error>.Ok(out);
}

/// Everything until the server's `close_notify`. A connection that ends any
/// other way is an `Err`, and what had arrived stays readable with `read`.
pub Result<bytes, io.Error> Conn.read_all() {
    bytes out = pending.substr(offset, pending.size());
    bytes none = [];
    pending = none;
    offset = 0;
    while (true) {
        match (next_chunk()) {
            case Ok(bytes chunk): {
                if (chunk.size() == 0) {
                    return Result<bytes, io.Error>.Ok(out);
                }
                out.extend(chunk);
            }
            case Err(io.Error read_error): {
                pending = out;
                return Result<bytes, io.Error>.Err(read_error);
            }
        }
    }
}

/// Octets up to and including the first `delimiter`, at most `limit` of
/// them; `net.Conn.read_until`'s three outcomes, told apart the same way.
pub Result<bytes, io.Error> Conn.read_until(bytes delimiter, int limit) {
    if (delimiter.size() == 0) {
        trap("read_until: the delimiter is empty");
    }
    if (limit < 1) {
        trap("read_until(" + limit.to_str() + "): the limit must be at least 1");
    }
    int last = delimiter[delimiter.size() - 1];
    bytes out = [];
    while (out.size() < limit) {
        if (offset == pending.size()) {
            match (next_chunk()) {
                case Ok(bytes chunk): {
                    if (chunk.size() == 0) {
                        break;
                    }
                    pending = chunk;
                    offset = 0;
                }
                case Err(io.Error read_error): {
                    pending = out;
                    offset = 0;
                    return Result<bytes, io.Error>.Err(read_error);
                }
            }
        }
        int next = pending[offset];
        offset = offset + 1;
        out.push(next);
        if (next == last && out.ends_with(delimiter)) {
            break;
        }
    }
    return Result<bytes, io.Error>.Ok(out);
}

/// Sends all of `data`, returning its size. An `Err` ends the connection.
pub Result<int, io.Error> Conn.write(bytes data) {
    if (closed) {
        trap("write on a closed connection");
    }
    if (failed) {
        return Result<int, io.Error>.Err(io.Error.Other(errno_of(failure)));
    }
    match (record.send_application(data)) {
        case Ok(int sent): {
            return Result<int, io.Error>.Ok(sent);
        }
        case Err(tls13record.Error record_error): {
            return Result<int, io.Error>.Err(fail(error_of_record(record_error)));
        }
    }
}

/// Tells the server the data is over with a `close_notify`, then closes the
/// socket. Closing twice is not an error.
pub Result<bool, io.Error> Conn.close() {
    return release();
}

Result<bool, io.Error> Conn.release() {
    if (closed) {
        return Result<bool, io.Error>.Ok(true);
    }
    closed = true;
    if (!failed) {
        Result<void, tls13record.Error> notified = record.send_close_notify();
    }
    match (record.close()) {
        case Ok(bool done): {
            return Result<bool, io.Error>.Ok(done);
        }
        case Err(tls13record.Error record_error): {
            return Result<bool, io.Error>.Err(io.Error.Other(ERRNO_IO));
        }
    }
}

void Conn.drop() {
    Result<bool, io.Error> ignored = release();
}

/// Why the connection stopped working, or `None` while it has not.
pub Option<Error> Conn.last_error() {
    if (failed) {
        return Option<Error>.Some(failure);
    }
    return Option<Error>.None;
}

/// The description of the fatal alert the server sent, or -1.
pub int Conn.peer_alert() {
    return record.received_alert();
}
